var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"using/#Using-a-cover","page":"Using a cover","text":"The examples on this page use the following program and its cover:\n\njulia> using DerivativeCovers, LinearAlgebra, MatrixCovers\n\njulia> f(x) = exp(x[1]) + x[1] * x[2] + x[2] * x[3] + x[3] * x[4];\n\njulia> x0 = [0.5, -1.2, 0.3, 0.8];\n\njulia> t, out = tape_function(f, x0);\n\njulia> h = cover_hessian(t, out).h_sum;\n\njulia> H = reduce(hcat, [hvp(t, out, e) for e in eachcol(Matrix(1.0I, 4, 4))]);   # for comparison","title":"Using a cover"},{"category":"section","location":"using/#What-a-cover-bounds","page":"Using a cover","text":"If abs.(H) .<= h .* h', then:\n\neach entry satisfies abs(H[i,j]) ≤ h[i] * h[j];\neach Hessian-vector product satisfies abs.(H * d) .≤ h .* (h' * abs.(d));\nthe scaled Hessian Hhat = H ./ (h .* h') has entries in [-1, 1], so its spectral radius is at most the number of nonzeros in the densest row of H, and in particular at most n.\n\nThe last is a statement about Hhat rather than H. When the inputs have different units, the entries of H have different units too, and the eigenvalue equation H * v = λ * v cannot be satisfied by a scalar λ with a single unit. Hhat is dimensionless, and because the cover transforms with the units (see Scale covariance), its eigenvalues do not depend on them.\n\njulia> d = [1.0, -2.0, 0.5, 0.0];\n\njulia> all(abs.(H * d) .<= h .* (h' * abs.(d)))\ntrue\n\njulia> Hhat = H ./ (h .* h');\n\njulia> maximum(abs, eigvals(Hhat)) <= maximum(count(!iszero, H; dims=2))\ntrue","title":"What a cover bounds"},{"category":"section","location":"using/#Scale-invariant-quantities","page":"Using a cover","text":"When the inputs are rescaled, x → D*x, the cover transforms as h → inv(D)*h (see Scale covariance). Quantities formed by dividing by the cover are therefore independent of the units of the inputs:\n\nthe scaled Hessian Hhat = H ./ (h .* h'), whose entries lie in [-1, 1];\nthe scaled gradient g ./ h, whose norm serves as a convergence test;\nthe scaled step h .* p, in terms of which the Newton equation H * p = -g becomes Hhat * (h .* p) = -(g ./ h).","title":"Scale-invariant quantities"},{"category":"section","location":"using/#Regularizing-an-indefinite-Hessian","page":"Using a cover","text":"Modified Newton methods and Tikhonov regularization shift the Hessian to make it positive definite. A shift by a multiple of the identity, H + τ*I, does not respect units: the same τ perturbs each variable by an amount that depends on its units. The diagonal of the cover respects them. The shift H + τ * Diagonal(h .^ 2) is positive semidefinite when τ ≥ -eigmin(Hhat), so any upper bound on the spectral radius of Hhat gives a sufficient τ.\n\nThe number of nonzeros in the densest row of H (see What a cover bounds) is such a bound. A tighter bound comes from scaled_spectral_radius, which estimates the spectral radius of Hhat by power iteration using Hessian-vector products, without forming H:\n\njulia> λ = DerivativeCovers.scaled_spectral_radius(t, out, h);\n\njulia> round(λ; digits=3)\n0.559\n\njulia> isposdef(H + 1.1 * λ * Diagonal(h .^ 2))\ntrue\n\nPower iteration approaches the spectral radius from below, so the estimate is inflated by a safety factor (here 1.1) before use. When H is available explicitly, the smallest sufficient τ is -eigmin(Hhat).","title":"Regularizing an indefinite Hessian"},{"category":"section","location":"using/#Tightening-a-cover","page":"Using a cover","text":"If the spectral radius of Hhat is λ < 1, then every entry of Hhat is at most λ in magnitude, and sqrt(λ) * h is also a cover. The power-iteration estimate of λ needs the same safety factor:\n\njulia> hs = sqrt(1.1 * λ) .* h;\n\njulia> round(max_coverage_ratio(h, H); digits=3), round(max_coverage_ratio(hs, H); digits=3)\n(0.449, 0.73)\n\nmax_coverage_ratio, from MatrixCovers, is the largest value of abs(H[i,j]) / (h[i] * h[j]); values closer to 1 indicate a tighter cover (see Tightness).","title":"Tightening a cover"},{"category":"section","location":"using/#Jacobians","page":"Using a cover","text":"For a Jacobian cover (a, b), the scale-invariant matrix is Jhat = J ./ (a .* b'), computed by equilibrate. The Jacobian counterparts of the functions above are scaled_singular_value, which computes the largest singular value of Jhat (by power iteration with jvp and vjp when given a tape). If σ < 1, then (sqrt(σ) * a, sqrt(σ) * b) is also a cover.\n\njulia> using DerivativeCovers\n\njulia> r(x) = [x[1] * x[2], exp(x[1]) - x[3], x[2] + 2x[3]];\n\njulia> t, outs = tape_function(r, [0.5, 2.0, -1.0]);\n\njulia> jc = cover_jacobian(t, outs);\n\njulia> σ = DerivativeCovers.scaled_singular_value(t, outs, jc.a, jc.b_sum);\n\njulia> round(σ; digits=3)\n0.866\n\njulia> a, b = sqrt(1.1 * σ) .* jc.a, sqrt(1.1 * σ) .* jc.b_sum;","title":"Jacobians"},{"category":"section","location":"using/#Matrix-free-products","page":"Using a cover","text":"The tape also supports exact products with the derivative matrices, each in one or two sweeps over the tape (O(L) time and memory):\n\nhvp: Hessian-vector product H * v;\njvp: Jacobian-vector product J * v;\nvjp: vector-Jacobian product J' * u.\n\nThe mutating forms hvp!, jvp!, and vjp! write the result into a supplied array.","title":"Matrix-free products"},{"category":"section","location":"tightness/#Tightness","page":"Tightness","text":"Every cover computed by this package is valid, but some are much looser than others. This page describes how to measure looseness and where it comes from.","title":"Tightness"},{"category":"section","location":"tightness/#Measuring-tightness","page":"Tightness","text":"When the Hessian H is available, max_coverage_ratio from MatrixCovers computes the largest ratio abs(H[i,j]) / (h[i] * h[j]) over the nonzero entries of H. A value of at most 1 confirms that h is a cover, and values closer to 1 indicate a tighter cover. The ratio reflects only the entry on which the cover is tightest, however: if r = max_coverage_ratio(h, H), then sqrt(r) * h has ratio exactly 1, however loose it remains elsewhere. For a Jacobian cover (a, b), the ratio is max_coverage_ratio(a, b, J).\n\nA reference cover computed from H itself, such as symcover(H) from MatrixCovers, allows a comparison entry by entry, which separates a uniform excess from a nonuniform one.","title":"Measuring tightness"},{"category":"section","location":"tightness/#Where-looseness-comes-from","page":"Tightness","text":"","title":"Where looseness comes from"},{"category":"section","location":"tightness/#The-rank-one-form","page":"Tightness","text":"A rank-one bound cannot match every matrix. A Jacobian with two blocks of large entries, for example, forces any rank-one cover to be loose on the small entries that join them. This floor depends only on the matrix, not on the program.","title":"The rank-one form"},{"category":"section","location":"tightness/#The-structure-of-the-program","page":"Tightness","text":"The covers are computed from the operations of the program, not from the matrix, so different programs for the same function can give different covers. A contribution reaches an input along every path through the program, and contributions that reach the same input along several paths are summed rather than allowed to cancel. Programs whose paths reconverge often therefore give looser covers.\n\nThe quadratic problem family of the companion package DerivativeCoverProblems writes the same quadratic form x^mathsf TAx2 as three different programs:\n\njulia> using DerivativeCovers, DerivativeCoverProblems, MatrixCovers\n\njulia> function ratios(form)\n           p = quadratic(6; rank=2, form)\n           H = exact_hessian(p)\n           hsum = cover_hessian(p.f, p.x0).h_sum\n           hquad = cover_hessian(WithDiagnostics(), p.f, p.x0).h_quad\n           return round.((max_coverage_ratio(hsum, H), max_coverage_ratio(hquad, H)); digits=3)\n       end;\n\njulia> ratios(:entrywise)   # sum_ij A[i,j] * x[i] * x[j] / 2\n(0.051, 0.332)\n\njulia> ratios(:matvec)      # x' * (A * x) / 2\n(0.145, 0.397)\n\njulia> ratios(:factored)    # sum((W' * x) .^ 2) / 2, with A = W * W'\n(0.879, 1.0)\n\nThe entrywise form sends each of the n^2 entries of A through its own product, and all of them reconverge on the inputs. The factored form has only rank nonlinear operations, which do not overlap. The quadrature merge recovers part of the difference.\n\nWithout the Hessian, the ratio h_sum ./ h_var of the two vectors in a HessianCover estimates how much the contributions to each coordinate overlap: it is 1 when each coordinate receives a single contribution and grows with the number of overlapping contributions.","title":"The structure of the program"},{"category":"section","location":"tightness/#Lost-cancellation","page":"Tightness","text":"The construction loses tightness wherever it takes absolute values of terms that would have cancelled. This happens in two places. The intermediate gradients carry the influence of each input forward through the program, and the adjoints carry the sensitivity of the output backward. Either can be computed with signs or in absolute-value arithmetic. The default cover_hessian keeps the adjoint signs and bounds the intermediate gradients, which is what lets it run in a single O(L) sweep; forward_cover does the opposite (see Propagating a cover forward). A difference of nearly equal terms, such as exp(x) - 1 - x near zero, has an exact intermediate gradient much smaller than its absolute-value bound; a value that reaches the output along paths with opposite signs has an adjoint much smaller than its absolute-value bound. When the program is small enough to form H, a cover computed from H itself, such as symcover(H), loses neither.","title":"Lost cancellation"},{"category":"section","location":"tightness/#Test-problems","page":"Tightness","text":"The quadratic family used above is one of several in DerivativeCoverProblems. Each generator returns a CoverProblem (or, for vector-valued programs, a JacobianProblem) containing the program, an evaluation point, and, where available, the exact Hessian:\n\njulia> using DerivativeCovers, DerivativeCoverProblems\n\njulia> p = quadratic(6; rank=2, form=:factored);\n\njulia> c = cover_hessian(p.f, p.x0);\n\njulia> H = exact_hessian(p);\n\njulia> all(abs.(H) .<= c.h_sum .* c.h_sum')\ntrue\n\nfamilies returns a standard set of generators that differ in how strongly the paths of the program reconverge. rescale and logscales change the units of a problem's inputs, for testing covariance. Problems with cancellation in the intermediate gradients or adjoints include cancellation, adjoint_cancellation, and converged_inner_solve.","title":"Test problems"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Internals","page":"Internals","text":"This page documents the sweeps, partitions, and intermediate quantities used to implement the public API. They can be called with the DerivativeCovers. prefix, but they are not part of the supported API and may change without notice.\n\nDerivativeCovers also adds methods for recorded tapes to several functions of MatrixCovers, such as gramcover. Those functions belong to MatrixCovers, so load that package to call them without qualification.\n\nPages = [\"internals.md\"]","title":"Internals"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.DeclaredScales","page":"Internals","text":"DeclaredScales(w, t)\n\nThe two node channels a block may ask for beyond the values: sensitivity_scales seeded with the input scales w, and the live_nodes flags. Each is built by the first block that asks for it and shared by every block after.\n\nDeferring the sweeps is what makes them free on a program that never needs them. Deciding in advance would take its own O(L) pass over the recorded blocks, which on a tape whose blocks all keep a usable value pair costs about as much as the cover sweep it precedes — so every program would pay for the possibility of a fallback none of its blocks reach.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.DeclaredScales"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.DeferredScale","page":"Internals","text":"DeferredScale(store, index)\n\nA declared scale named but not yet computed: slot index of store, resolved by declared_scale when a block first reaches the fallback.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.DeferredScale"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.ElementalSite","page":"Internals","text":"ElementalSite(val, operands)\n\nThe CoverSite of an elemental in a forward propagation. Without a tape to number the elemental, only its value and operand values identify it, so those are what the site reports.\n\noperands is held as given and read only when an error is raised, so naming a site costs nothing on the path that succeeds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.ElementalSite"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.ForwardChannels","page":"Internals","text":"ForwardChannels{T, S, M, SM}\n\nThe two vector channels an FVar carries beyond its value and its declared scale: the intermediate gradient g = ∇v (storage S, entries T), and the squared cover G (storage SM, entries M = boundtype(T)) maintaining sqrt.(G) * sqrt.(G)' >= magnitude.(∇²v) entrywise.\n\nG is squared rather than rooted because the recursion that maintains it is linear in the parents' G, which is what keeps a per-node re-merge from compounding with depth.\n\nThe channels live in the FVar's own fields; channels assembles this view of them on demand, and it costs nothing once inlined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.ForwardChannels"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.ForwardContext","page":"Internals","text":"ForwardContext{T, S, M, SM}\n\nState shared by every variable in a forward propagation: the degeneracy threshold local covers split at, whether each local cover is verified, and the scratch a node writes through while assembling its cover.\n\nS is the storage of the intermediate-gradient channel, one entry of T per input, and SM that of the cover channel, one entry of M = boundtype(T) per input. forward_cover derives both from the point it is given, so a Vector input propagates in Vectors and a StaticVector input in StaticVectors. zerogradient and zerochannel are zeros of the two storages, which fresh channels are copied from; the input variables share zerochannel as their cover channel. scratch is the atom accumulator, written in place when SM is mutable and left alone when it is not; and rho holds the slot weights of the elemental being covered.\n\nIt is a reference type so that every variable of the propagation holds a pointer to the one context: storing an immutable into a variable's field would copy it, at one allocation per node. Identity is then also what === compares, which is what distinguishes two propagations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.ForwardContext"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.ForwardScale","page":"Internals","text":"ForwardScale(sigma, live)\n\nThe declared channel of one slot of a forward propagation: the scale sigma that stands in for a parent value carrying none, and whether the slot's intermediate gradient is identically zero.\n\nLiveness travels separately from sigma because sigma cannot report it. It is a sum of products of first partials, so it underflows on a long chain of small factors while the intermediate gradient it bounds is still there, and a slot dropped on that evidence would leave a real Hessian entry uncovered. The flag is carried by the same recursion live_nodes runs over a tape — a slot is live when some parent is live and reaches it through a nonzero partial — so both backends drop exactly the same entries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.ForwardScale"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.MaxMerge","page":"Internals","text":"MaxMerge <: MergeRule\n\nb_j = max_k Ũ_kj/a_k, the exact minimal b for the given a. Non-smooth, so its marginal alternation can settle into a limit cycle: the weights keep changing by a fixed amount every round, which no larger budget reduces. balance_cover reports that as a stall.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.MaxMerge"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.MergeRule","page":"Internals","text":"MergeRule\n\nHow balance_cover's b-step combines a column of the scaled surrogate into one input weight: given the entries Ũ_kj/a_k over the outputs k, it returns b_j. Every rule that dominates each of its terms yields a valid cover, so the choice trades tightness, never validity.\n\nSumMerge, QuadMerge and MaxMerge are the p = 1, 2, ∞ members of one family, so a parameterized member (a p-norm merge) is reachable through the same b-step without an API break. They are types rather than names so that the b-step's doubly-nested loop specializes on the choice, and so that the non-convergence hint can dispatch per rule.\n\nSignAveragedMerge is the exception: it runs the whole alternation, not only the final b-step, in quadrature on R, and the pair it returns is a sign-averaged cover, which does not cover J.\n\narule, the other strategy axis of balance_cover, is a Symbol instead, because it is closed: see that function's docstring.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.MergeRule"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.QuadMerge","page":"Internals","text":"QuadMerge <: MergeRule\n\nb_j = √(∑_k (Ũ_kj/a_k)²), combining the same terms in quadrature.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.QuadMerge"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.SignAveragedMerge","page":"Internals","text":"SignAveragedMerge <: MergeRule\n\nThe sign-averaged pair: balance_cover's marginal alternation run in quadrature on R (quadrature_surrogate),\n\na_k ← sqrt((1/ρ_k) ∑_j R_kj² / b_j²),    b_j ← sqrt((1/γ_j) ∑_k R_kj² / a_k²),\n\nfollowed by the final b-step b_j = sqrt(∑_k R_kj² / a_k²). Squared, this is the SumMerge alternation applied to the squared channel Ũ⁽²⁾ = R.^2 with factors (a², b²), the same targets and round budget, and the squared start. The iteration works with a, b and R themselves and accumulates every sum of squares with hypot, so it never forms the square of a Jacobian-scale quantity, and the pair is representable wherever the plain pair is. On a tape each half-step is one sweep combining local partials in quadrature, so a round costs O(L) and R is never formed; the final b-step equals gramvariance(t, outidxs, w) with w_k = 1/a_k² on the live outputs and zero on the others, where those squares are representable.\n\nThe pair is a sign-averaged cover, not a cover of J. The final b-step gives every live column ∑_k R_kj² / (a_k b_j)² = 1, so a_k b_j >= R_kj, which is what check = true verifies. But R <= Ũ, with equality only where an entry has a single path, so a_k b_j can fall below Ũ_kj, and below abs(J_kj) where several paths add with the same sign. If each parent slot's local partial carries an independent, equiprobable sign, R_kj² = E[J_kj²], and at convergence the pair standardizes the signed Jacobian in expectation: the normalized iterate (a, b ./ sqrt.(γ)) has expected row sums of squares ∑_j E[J_kj²] / (a_k² b_j² / γ_j) = ρ_k and column sums γ_j, and the returned b has expected column sums of squares 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.SignAveragedMerge"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.SumMerge","page":"Internals","text":"SumMerge <: MergeRule\n\nb_j = ∑_k Ũ_kj/a_k, the loosest merge and, with SignAveragedMerge, the only one the tape form can run as O(L) sweeps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.SumMerge"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.UndecidedAbs","page":"Internals","text":"UndecidedAbs(x::CoverVar)\n\nabs(x) for a non-constant x whose sign is not decided (see decided_sign). It records nothing. A constant power UndecidedAbs(x)^p with p >= 2 records |x|^p as one node on x; every recorder throws a DomainError when handed one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.UndecidedAbs"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers._flow_targets-Tuple{Tape, Any, DerivativeCovers.LiveInfo}","page":"Internals","text":"_flow_targets(t::Tape, outidxs, li::LiveInfo) -> (r, c)\n\nRow and column targets from the two flow weightings of the live slot graph, mixed so that neither side dominates: r = ρ_fwd/nrow + ρ_rev/ncol and c = γ_fwd/nrow + γ_rev/ncol, with nrow the live output positions and ncol the live inputs. Marginals are linear in the weight matrix, so the mixture is itself the marginal pair of a nonnegative matrix with Ũ's pattern, which is what makes the targets realizable.\n\nTwo of the four marginals are constant by construction — forward flow reaches every live node with mass 1, and reverse flow leaves every live input with mass 1 — so only two sweeps run. O(L), forming no matrix; every weight is at most 1, so nothing can overflow. The two totals are 2 each, to roundoff.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers._flow_targets"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers._structural_pattern-Tuple{Tape, Any, DerivativeCovers.LiveInfo}","page":"Internals","text":"_structural_pattern(t::Tape, outidxs, li::LiveInfo) -> P::Matrix{UInt64}\n\nThe structural sparsity pattern of Ũ packed 64 inputs to a word: bit (j - 1) % 64 of P[k, cld(j, 64)] is set exactly when output position k reaches input j along a path of nonzero local derivatives. cld(n, 64) integer passes over the tape and m * cld(n, 64) words, both small beside the m * n surrogate this is compared against.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers._structural_pattern"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers._support_counts-Tuple{Tape, Any, DerivativeCovers.LiveInfo}","page":"Internals","text":"_support_counts(t::Tape, outidxs, li::LiveInfo) -> (r, c)\n\nExact reachability counts: r[k] live inputs reaching output position k, c[j] live output positions reached by input j — the row and column nonzero counts of Ũ, obtained without forming it. Word-parallel bit propagation over the live slot graph: cld(n, 64) forward passes and cld(m, 64) reverse passes, each O(L) integer work.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers._support_counts"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.abs_forward-Tuple{Tape, AbstractVector}","page":"Internals","text":"abs_forward(t::Tape, theta::AbstractVector) -> s\n\nOne forward sweep in absolute-value arithmetic, the transpose of abs_reverse: s[k] = theta[k] + sum_i abs(∂φ_k/∂v_{p_i}) * s[p_i] over the parents p_i of node k, in tape order. Seeding theta[j] = 1 at a single input j gives, at every node, the absolute sensitivity of that node to input j — the j-th column of the surrogate Ũ read at the output nodes — and the sweep is linear in the seeds, so any weighted combination of inputs costs the same one O(L) traversal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.abs_forward"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.abs_reverse-Tuple{Tape, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Internals","text":"abs_reverse(t::Tape, Theta::AbstractMatrix) -> Abar\n\nVector-mode form of abs_reverse: each row of Theta is an independent seed vector over the nodes (columns), and all rows are propagated in one traversal. Abar[:, a] for an input a holds that input's value for every channel. The seeds are nonnegative magnitudes, as group_seeds returns them in boundtype of the tape's element type, and the sweep computes in their type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.abs_reverse"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.abs_reverse-Tuple{Tape, AbstractVector}","page":"Internals","text":"abs_reverse(t::Tape, theta) -> abar\n\nOne reverse sweep in absolute-value arithmetic: abar[j] = theta[j] + sum_{k : j in P_k} magnitude(∂φ_k/∂v_j) * abar[k].\n\nWith theta from local_covers, abar[1:t.ninputs] is the sum-merge cover — the whole merge in a single gradient-cost sweep, because sum_k s_k = sum_j theta[j] * utilde_j is linear in the seeds.\n\nThe sweep computes in the element type of theta, so seeds in boundtype give a sweep that rounds as the tape asks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.abs_reverse"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.balance_cover-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Tape{T}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"balance_cover(t::Tape, outidxs; kwargs...) -> (; a, b, iters, delta, roots, nties)\nbalance_cover(r, x0::AbstractVector; kwargs...)\nbalance_cover(Utilde::AbstractMatrix; kwargs...)\n\nOutput and input weights covering the absolute-sensitivity surrogate: a[k] * b[j] >= Ũ_kj, and so >= abs(J_kj), for every merge except SignAveragedMerge.\n\nBy default the weights come from a scale-covariant start, rounds rounds of a marginal alternation toward row and column targets the sparsity pattern makes realizable, and a final MergeRule b-step for the a those rounds reached. The b-step is what makes the pair a cover, so the result covers whatever the rounds did or did not settle. The accompanying manuscript derives the weighting rules and their convergence; the keywords below say what each one does without it.\n\nKeywords\n\nstart = :covariant: products on the support then transform exactly under a rescaling of the inputs and outputs — and, on the tape path, the intermediates — at every round count. start = :equal starts at a = 1 instead, which is permutation equivariant at every round count but covariant only in the limit. Both reach the same fixed point.\na0: starting weights of your own, positive on every live output and zero on the others. Combining it with an explicit start is an error.\ntargets: :flow (tape default; O(L); maximizes a flow-weighted mean log-tightness, which can be noticeably looser than :support when outputs differ widely in the number of paths reaching them), :support (matrix default; maximizes the geometric-mean tightness of the sum-merge cover; on a tape it costs cld(n, 64) + cld(m, 64) bit passes), an explicit (r, c), or :uniform for unit row and column sums. :uniform needs the pattern to have total support — every nonzero entry on some positive diagonal — and without it the weights drift, separating without bound while every iterate still covers, so a longer run returns a looser cover. Applies to arule = :marginal only, and :flow needs a tape.\nrounds = 4: run exactly this many rounds, with no convergence test and no error. 0 merges the start as it is, without computing targets, and reports delta = Inf. nothing runs to delta <= rtol instead and throws if maxiter rounds do not reach it; naming maxiter or rtol and no rounds asks for that too. Such a run overrelaxes the alternation toward targets, in log space with a factor adapted to the observed contraction, which reaches the same fixed point in far fewer rounds on slowly converging patterns; a fixed budget always runs the plain rounds.\nmaxiter, rtol: bound and tolerance of a rounds = nothing run. maxiter = nothing picks 1000 rounds for arule = :marginal and 10000 for :geomean. Passing maxiter together with a rounds count is an error.\nmerge: the final b-step, SumMerge (default), QuadMerge or MaxMerge. SignAveragedMerge instead runs the whole alternation in quadrature on R and returns a sign-averaged pair, which does not cover J; the matrix form then takes R (quadrature_surrogate) in place of Ũ, and check verifies a_k b_j >= R_kj.\narule: :marginal (default) alternates marginal_weights with the b-step; :geomean fits log Ũ_kj ≈ log a_k + log b_j instead and prescribes no targets.\nroot, cgiters: passed to tape_potentials for the tape-potential start. Naming them on a call that does not reach that start is an error.\ncheck = true: verify the returned pair against the surrogate.\n\nReturns\n\na and b by output position and input, iters rounds run, delta the quantity the convergence test applies to (the gauge-invariant product change max_kj |log((a_k b_j)_new/(a_k b_j)_old)|, or the relative row-marginal residual max_k |∑_j Ũ_kj/(a_k b_j r_k) - 1| for non-uniform targets), and roots, nties from tape_potentials when the tape-potential start was used, nothing otherwise. A zero row of Ũ gets a[k] = 0 and a zero column b[j] = 0. With SignAveragedMerge(), delta is measured on the pair itself and R: the product change is that of a_k b_j, and the residual is max_k |sqrt(∑_j R_kj²/b_j²) / (a_k sqrt(r_k)) - 1|.\n\nCost\n\nThe tape form with merge = SumMerge() or SignAveragedMerge() and arule = :marginal runs as O(L) sweeps per round and never forms Ũ. Every other combination, and check = true, forms it (or R) at O(m*L).\n\nExamples\n\njulia> r(x) = [x[1] * x[2], exp(x[1]) - x[3], x[2] + 2x[3]];\n\njulia> bal = DerivativeCovers.balance_cover(r, [0.5, 2.0, -1.0]);\n\njulia> jc = cover_jacobian(WithDiagnostics(), r, [0.5, 2.0, -1.0]; a = bal.a);\n\njulia> bal.iters, all(jc.b_max .<= bal.b)\n(4, true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.balance_cover"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.balanced_split-NTuple{5, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"balanced_split(c11, c12, c22, z1, z2) -> (rho1, rho2)\n\nRank-1 cover of an off-diagonal-active 2×2 block [c11 c12; c12 c22], which requires c12^2 > c11*c22 (see offdiag_active). Returns rho1 >= sqrt(c11) and rho2 >= sqrt(c22) with rho1*rho2 == c12: the cover is exactly tight on the off-diagonal, so no cover of the block is smaller in both entries.\n\nThose constraints leave a one-parameter family. The scale references z1, z2 — one per parent, supplied by split_block: the parent value magnitudes wherever they carry a scale, the weighted absolute sensitivities where they do not — pick a member, giving rho_i the units of sqrt(f)/[v_{p_i}] and making the cover scale-covariant. The result is continuous in all five arguments except where a vanishing scale reference meets a degenerate block; see the extended help.\n\nExtended help\n\nThe three constraints confine rho1 to the segment [sqrt(c11), c12/sqrt(c22)] — nonempty precisely because c12^2 > c11*c22 — and fix rho2 = c12/rho1. Every returned cover lies on this segment, which is what makes it entrywise unimprovable: a competitor smaller in both entries would have rho1*rho2 < c12.\n\nThe position along the segment is the point that equalizes the value-weighted scales, rho1*z1 == rho2*z2, clamped to the segment when that point falls outside. Covariance follows: under a rescaling v_i -> d_i*v_i of the intermediate variables, c_ij -> c_ij/(d_i*d_j) and z_i -> d_i*z_i, so both endpoints and the equalizing point scale as 1/d_1; clamp is positively homogeneous, so rho_1 -> rho_1/d_1 and rho_2 -> rho_2/d_2.\n\nClamping is also what keeps the result continuous as a parent value vanishes: the equalizing point runs off to an endpoint, and the endpoint absorbs it. Nothing computes those limits separately, because floating-point arithmetic already carries them. sqrt(c12*z2/z1) is Inf at z1 == 0 and 0 at z2 == 0, which clamp resolves to the upper and the lower endpoint; c12/sqrt(c22) is Inf at c22 == 0, placing the upper endpoint there. Only z1 == z2 == 0 leaves a NaN that no clamp resolves, and that is what reaches the fallback.\n\nThat reasoning needs the endpoint being approached to be a positive finite scale, and it is not when the corresponding diagonal entry vanishes: c22 == 0 puts the upper endpoint at infinity, c11 == 0 puts the lower endpoint at zero. Nor is there any limit to take when both values vanish at once, since the equalizing point then depends on the ratio z2/z1 alone. In those cases the geometric mean of the endpoints is used, which is covariant and permutation-equivariant.\n\nSo the result is continuous except at three families of inputs, where it is the ratio z2/z1 that carries the information and the ratio is unavailable:\n\nz1 == 0 with c22 == 0,\nz2 == 0 with c11 == 0,\nz1 == z2 == 0, for any block.\n\nA multiplication node with a vanishing operand has c11 == c22 == 0 and meets all three at once. Among the other elementals of overloads.jl, a quotient u/w meets the first at u == 0, since c22 = 2u/w^3 vanishes with the numerator, and x^y meets the second at a zero exponent, where c11 == 0 and z2 == 0 while c22 > 0.\n\nThe continuity above is continuity in the values with the block held fixed. At those two elementals the block moves with the value: the diagonal entry that bounds the feasible segment is proportional to the vanishing value, so the endpoint the clamp settles on moves too, and the seed of the slot whose value vanishes grows like |value|^(-1/2). The split stays covariant and valid, but the cover grows without bound as the value approaches zero while the derivatives it covers stay finite, and at the zero itself the block falls into the families above and the cover jumps to a finite one.\n\nThese zero-reference cases concern the value channel, and they are exactly the ones value_bounds_split detects: split_block substitutes the declared scales there, and a declared scale vanishes only at a slot whose intermediate gradient is zero, which cover_slots! covers diagonally instead of splitting. So a split reaches this function with a pair that is not degenerate, and a pair carrying a ratio reaches the geometric mean only when c12*z2/z1 leaves the range of the element type at a block whose feasible segment has a degenerate endpoint. There the equalizing point is recomputed from the separated exponents, which represents it whenever it is representable at all; see split_fallback. A split that is genuinely out of range throws, since every pair returned here is a cover. Entries that are already non-finite are returned as IEEE arithmetic gives them, which is what check=false asks for.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.balanced_split"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.channels-Union{Tuple{FVar{T, S, M, SM}}, Tuple{SM}, Tuple{M}, Tuple{S}, Tuple{T}} where {T, S, M, SM}","page":"Internals","text":"channels(x::FVar) -> ForwardChannels\n\nThe intermediate-gradient and cover channels behind x. A constant has neither: every elemental folds a constant operand into one that propagates, so reaching here with a constant means a caller bypassed that folding.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.channels"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.check_input_scales-Tuple{Any, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"check_input_scales(w, ninputs::Integer) -> w\n\nValidate declared input scales: nothing for unit scales, or one strictly positive finite entry per input.\n\nPositivity is not a formality. The declared scales stand in for the parent values at blocks whose values carry none, so a zero entry would blank a live slot's scale, leaving its split with no ratio to read and, at a block whose feasible segment is degenerate too, no cover to return.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.check_input_scales"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.cyclic_partition-Tuple{Tape, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"cyclic_partition(t::Tape, ngroups::Integer) -> groups\n\nAssign node k of t to group 1 + (k-1) % ngroups, a node-granular partition for cover_hybrid.\n\nLooseness comes from summing contributions whose covers overlap, and overlapping contributions are typically siblings — the many product terms of a quadratic form, the fan-out of a reduction — which a tape lists consecutively. Dealing them round-robin therefore spreads them across groups, where quadrature combines them. Binning by depth instead (see level_partition) puts every sibling in the same group, so on such programs it recovers little of the quadrature merge.\n\nSiblings absorbed into a single array primitive are siblings this partition cannot separate, since every atom of a node lands in the node's group. Dealing atoms instead — cover_hybrid's integer form, see group_seeds — spreads them whether the program packs them into one primitive or spells them out.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.cyclic_partition"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.declared_scale-Tuple{DerivativeCovers.SplitScale}","page":"Internals","text":"declared_scale(x) -> sigma\n\nThe declared scale behind one slot's reference: the absolute sensitivity that stands in for a parent value carrying no scale of its own.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.declared_scale"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.dependency_sweep-Tuple{Tape, AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"Internals","text":"dependency_sweep(t::Tape, theta, theta2) -> (abar, vbar)\n\nThe staged form's propagation of the hard and squared channels, one reverse sweep each:\n\nabar[j] = theta[j]  + sum_{k : j in P_k} magnitude(∂φ_k/∂v_j)   * abar[k]\nvbar[j] = theta2[j] + sum_{k : j in P_k} magnitude(∂φ_k/∂v_j)^2 * vbar[k]\n\nThe hard channel is abs_reverse: seeded with theta from local_covers, its value at the inputs is the sum merge. The squared channel squares both the seeds and the coefficients, so it collects the same path contributions in quadrature; seeded with the matching theta2 from squared_seeds, sqrt.(vbar) at the inputs is the sign-averaged cover, an estimate of the quadrature merge from below. Both channels hold magnitudes: vbar here is the squared channel, not an adjoint.\n\ncover_hessian forms both channels within its single fused sweep instead; this form seeds them independently.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.dependency_sweep"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.dispatchcurvature-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, DerivativeCovers.Curvature, Type{T}}} where {F, T}","page":"Internals","text":"dispatchcurvature(f, C::Curvature, ::Type{T})\n\nCall f(C), branching on the built-in Curvature forms over the element type T first.\n\nTape stores its curvatures in a container with abstract element type, so an interface method invoked on one is a dynamic dispatch — once per node per sweep, which is a large fraction of the sweep's cost. Testing the built-in forms by isa first lets the compiler resolve those calls statically. A form defined elsewhere falls through to the same call, dispatched the ordinary way: this is an optimization for the common cases, not a restriction on which forms are allowed.\n\nT is the element type of the tape the curvature came from, which is the one the elementals of overloads.jl record in; a DeclaredCurvature holds magnitudes, so its form is resolved over magtype(T). A curvature over some other element or matrix type is correct here, just not statically resolved.\n\nC is @nospecialized because specializing on it would defeat the point: a caller that knows the concrete form would compile an instance in which every branch below but one is dead, having already paid the dispatch this ladder exists to avoid. One instance per call site and element type, branching at run time, is the intended shape.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.dispatchcurvature"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.group_seeds-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Tape{T}, AbstractVector, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"group_seeds(t::Tape, rhos, groups::AbstractVector{<:Integer}) -> Theta\ngroup_seeds(t::Tape, rhos, ngroups::Integer) -> Theta\n\nSeed weights for the vector-mode reverse sweep, Theta[g, j] = sum over the contributions in group g of the weight each places on node j. Channels index the rows so that the weights of one contribution are contiguous.\n\nThe two forms differ in what a group holds. groups[k] assigns tape node k, keeping all of its atoms together, and maximum(groups) sets the channel count. An integer ngroups assigns atoms (see atom_slots): they are numbered in tape order and dealt round-robin, so a node exposing several atoms spreads them over several groups. The channel count is ngroups, capped at natoms(t) since further channels would stay empty.\n\nSince a node's atoms partition its slots, both forms deposit the same total weight and differ only in how they distribute it over channels. Dealing atoms makes the distribution independent of how many atoms one elemental carries, so an array primitive that absorbs several atoms into one node still has them spread across channels. Which atoms share a channel remains a matter of the order they appear in.\n\nWith rhos from local_covers, column j collects exactly the seeds that abs_reverse propagates into the group partial sums. Theta is in boundtype of the tape's element type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.group_seeds"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.hessian_bound-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Tape{T}, AbstractVector, AbstractVector}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"hessian_bound(t::Tape, vbar, utilde) -> B\n\nThe elementwise bound B = sum_k magnitude(vbar[k]) * sum_{i,j} magnitude(C^k[i,j]) * utilde_i * utilde_j', which satisfies B >= abs.(H) entrywise, in boundtype of the tape's element type. Diagnostic only: B is n × n, and the two inequalities it discards — cancellation along paths (inside each utilde) and cancellation across elementals — are exactly the looseness this bound trades for its cost.\n\nEach node's term is accumulated by bound_contribution!, which every Curvature supplies in the form its structure allows. A DeclaredCurvature node contributes its declared bound s[i]*s[j] >= abs(C[i, j]), so B >= abs.(H) remains valid conditional on the declaration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.hessian_bound"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.intermediate_gradients-Union{Tuple{Tape{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"intermediate_gradients(t::Tape) -> u\n\nThe signed intermediate gradients: u[k][a] = ∂v_k/∂x_a, obtained by propagating u[k] = sum_{i in P_k} (∂φ_k/∂v_i) * u[i] from u[x_a] = e_a.\n\nThe signed counterpart of sensitivities, which runs the same recursion in absolute-value arithmetic and so returns a bound utilde >= abs.(u) rather than the derivatives themselves. The gap between the two is the cancellation that occurs along paths from an input to a node.\n\nCosts O(n * L) time and memory. Only first partials enter, so a declared sub-map node (see submapnode) propagates exactly like any other.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.intermediate_gradients"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.jacobian_surrogate-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Tape{T}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"jacobian_surrogate(t::Tape, outidxs::AbstractVector{<:Integer}) -> Utilde\n\nThe m × n surrogate of absolute sensitivities of the outputs outidxs with respect to the inputs of t: Utilde[k, j] ≥ |J_kj|, tight when no signed path cancellation occurs between output k and input j. Formed by one m-channel absolute-value reverse sweep seeded at the output nodes (see abs_reverse), so it costs O(m*L) — a diagnostic, like the Hessian bound B. A repeated entry in outidxs yields an identical row. The sweep runs in boundtype of the tape's element type and the result is returned in its magtype.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.jacobian_surrogate"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.level_partition-Tuple{Tape, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"level_partition(t::Tape, ngroups::Integer) -> groups\n\nAssign each node of t to one of ngroups groups by binning node_levels into contiguous depth bands. Nodes at the same depth always land in the same group, and when the tape is shallower than ngroups each depth gets a group of its own (leaving the surplus groups empty). Inputs carry no second-order contribution, so their group is irrelevant; they are assigned group 1.\n\nThis separates nodes along a chain but never separates siblings, which limits how much of the quadrature merge it can recover; cyclic_partition is the default for that reason.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.level_partition"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.live_nodes-Tuple{Tape}","page":"Internals","text":"live_nodes(t::Tape) -> live\n\nWhich nodes of t have an intermediate gradient that is not identically zero. live[k] is false exactly when every path from the inputs to node k passes through a recorded first partial that is exactly zero (whose magnitude is zero), and then ∇v_k is the zero vector in any arithmetic: every term of every path product carries an exact zero factor.\n\nThe test is on the recorded partials rather than on a propagated magnitude, so it neither underflows a live node to dead nor depends on the declared input scales.\n\nCosts one O(L) forward pass, paid once per cover and only by a program carrying a block whose off-diagonal is active.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.live_nodes"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.local_cover_slots-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Tape{T}, Integer, Real}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"local_cover_slots(t::Tape{T}, k::Integer, av; check::Bool=true) -> Union{Nothing,Vector{M}}\nlocal_cover_slots(ratio, t::Tape, k::Integer, av, scales; check::Bool=true)\n\nThe rank-1 cover of a single node, slot-aligned with its parents: nonnegative rho with rho[i]*rho[j] >= av*magnitude(C^k[i,j]) wherever both slots have a nonzero intermediate gradient (see dead_slot), and av the absolute adjoint magnitude(vbar[k]). rho and av are in M = magtype(T). Returns nothing when the node contributes no second-order term (av == 0, or a NoCurvature node). This is the per-node kernel shared by local_covers, which accumulates the results into (rhos, theta), and the CoverOnly sweep, which deposits them directly into the absolute adjoint channel. It reads the node's Curvature off the tape and forwards to cover_slots, which holds the construction for each form. check verifies the covering invariant on recorded nodes.\n\nThe first form takes the default split, resolving the threshold against the tape's element type. The second takes a resolved ratio together with scales = split_scales(w, t), both computed once by the caller and shared across nodes.\n\nThis is the tape's adapter onto cover_slots: it names the node with a TapeSite and gathers scales[t.parents[k]] into slot order, both lazily.\n\nA node whose covering entries are not finite is reported by check_finite_partials rather than by the invariant test.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.local_cover_slots"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.local_covers-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Tape{T}, AbstractVector}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"local_covers(t::Tape, vbar; check::Bool=true) -> (rhos, theta)\n\nPer-node rank-1 covers. For node k, rhos[k][i] >= 0 satisfies rhos[k][i] * rhos[k][j] >= c^k[i,j] where c^k[i,j] = magnitude(vbar[k]) * magnitude(C^k[i,j]), except at entries a dead_slot empties; nodes with no second-order contribution get an empty rhos[k]. theta[j] = sum_k rhos[k][i] collects the weight each node receives as a parent, which seeds the reverse sweep of abs_reverse; it is in boundtype of the tape's element type, so the sweep it seeds rounds as the tape asks.\n\nEach node's cover comes from cover_slots, dispatching on its Curvature. A DeclaredCurvature node (a declared sub-map output, see submapnode) brings its own rank-1 cover, and there is no recorded C to check it against, so check cannot audit those nodes — validity is conditional on the declaration.\n\nscales declares the input scales the split falls back on and ratio the threshold at which it does so; see split_block and split_ratio. check verifies the covering invariant at every recorded node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.local_covers"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.marginal_weights-Tuple{Tape, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractVector}","page":"Internals","text":"marginal_weights(t::Tape, outidxs::AbstractVector{<:Integer}, b::AbstractVector) -> a\nmarginal_weights(Utilde::AbstractMatrix, b::AbstractVector) -> a\n\nThe output weights a_k = ∑_j Ũ_kj / b_j — the row marginal of the surrogate weighted by the current input cover b. Each term of the sum already certifies its own entry (a_k b_j ≥ Ũ_kj), so (a, b) is a valid cover of the surrogate for any positive b.\n\nThis is the covariant output-weighting rule: under an output rescaling r → diag(c)*r it picks up exactly diag(c), and under an input rescaling x → diag(d)*x (with b → diag(d)⁻¹*b alongside) it is exactly invariant — each term Ũ_kj/b_j carries the units of r_k alone. The bare row marginal ∑_j Ũ_kj lacks the second property, and the value rule a_k = |r_k| degenerates wherever residuals vanish.\n\nThe tape method costs one O(L) abs_forward sweep seeded 1/b_j at the inputs, never forming Ũ. A zero b_j is accepted only for an input no output depends on: it contributes nothing to any marginal. A zero b_j on an input that does reach an output is an error — no output weighting can restore validity there.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.marginal_weights"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.maxpath_forward-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Tape{T}, AbstractVector}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"maxpath_forward(t::Tape, d) -> TopTwo\n\nForward (max, ×) sweep with per-input seeds 1 / d[l]: for every node k, the two largest values of U[k, l] / d[l] over inputs l, with their inputs, where U[k, l] is the largest product of absolute first partials along any path from input l to node k. An input with d[l] == 0 seeds nothing.\n\nO(L) time and memory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.maxpath_forward"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.maxpath_rowcorrelation-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Tape{T}, Integer, AbstractVector}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"maxpath_rowcorrelation(t::Tape, outidx::Integer, d; check=true, scales=nothing, ratio=nothing) -> m\n\nThe (max, ×) row statistic of the Hessian at outidx relative to the scales d:\n\nm[i] = max over atoms of smax[i] * max_{l != i} smax[l] / d[l] / d[i],\n\nwhere an atom's smax[l] = max_{slots} rho[slot] * U[parent, l] takes each slot along its largest path (U as in maxpath_forward) and the largest of its slots, and rho are the local covers of local_cover_slots under the same scales and ratio the cover sweeps use.\n\nThis is the max-merge counterpart of the largest normalized off-diagonal entry in row i of the quadrature merge's surrogate sum_atoms s_atom s_atom', and is at most that entry. It is exact for a sum of squared linear forms written with each input once per form. It is a statistic for starting a metric fit, not a bound, and not a cover. With d covariant (for instance a cover), m is invariant under rescaling of the inputs and of the output. A coordinate with d[i] == 0 neither seeds the forward sweep nor receives a value: m[i] = 0.\n\nTwo sweeps: the forward (max, ×) sweep, and one reverse sweep that carries the adjoint and the (max, ×) channel together, O(L) time and memory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.maxpath_rowcorrelation"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.merge_atoms-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Tape{T}, AbstractVector, AbstractVector}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"merge_atoms(t::Tape, rhos, u) -> (h_sum, h_quad)\n\nBoth merges of the cover atoms, from per-node cover slots rhos and an intermediate-gradient channel u. An atom's cover vector is s_atom = sum_{i in atom} rhos[k][i] * u[P_k[i]], and the merges are h_sum = sum_atoms s_atom and h_quad[i] = sqrt(sum_atoms s_atom[i]^2).\n\nu[j] must be nonnegative and dominate abs.(∇v_j) entrywise for the results to cover; both sensitivities and the magnitudes of intermediate_gradients qualify. The merges are in boundtype of the tape's element type. Which slots share an atom comes from atom_slots, and the loop depends on its partitioning the slots: a slot in no atom would lose its contribution, one named twice would be counted twice.\n\nCosts O(n * L) time and O(n) memory beyond u.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.merge_atoms"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.nls_partition-Tuple{Integer, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Internals","text":"nls_partition(N, sqidx; ngroups=nothing) -> groups\n\nNode partition for the residuals-as-groups hybrid merge of a least-squares loss tape with N nodes and residual-square nodes sqidx. Each residual square is placed in a group of its own (ngroups === nothing, one group per residual, giving the exact Gauss–Newton diagonal in quadrature) or spread cyclically across ngroups groups (a cheaper upper bound). Every other node — the residual-curvature subgraph — is placed in one further group, so its second-order contribution is summed there and combined in quadrature with the Gauss–Newton groups.\n\nThat last group is always occupied, so maximum(groups) - 1 recovers the number of residual groups even when ngroups exceeds the residual count.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.nls_partition"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.node_levels-Tuple{Tape}","page":"Internals","text":"node_levels(t::Tape) -> levels\n\nDepth of each node: 0 for the inputs, and 1 + maximum(levels[parents]) otherwise. Parents always precede their children on the tape, so one forward pass suffices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.node_levels"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.offdiag_active-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"offdiag_active(c11, c12, c22) -> Bool\n\nWhether the diagonal cover (sqrt(c11), sqrt(c22)) of the nonnegative block [c11 c12; c12 c22] falls short of its off-diagonal, i.e. whether c12^2 > c11*c22, in which case balanced_split is needed.\n\nThe test is c12 > sqrt(c11)*sqrt(c22), never the squared form: c12^2 underflows to zero for c12 below the square root of the smallest subnormal (about 1e-162 in Float64), which would misread a block with c11 == c22 == 0 and a tiny but nonzero cross term as diagonal and cover it with (0, 0). A long running product of small factors produces exactly such blocks. Square roots keep every representable entry inside the representable range, so this form neither underflows nor overflows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.offdiag_active"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.quadrature_surrogate-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Tape{T}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"quadrature_surrogate(t::Tape, outidxs::AbstractVector{<:Integer}) -> R\n\nThe m × n quadrature counterpart of jacobian_surrogate: R[k, j] is the 2-norm, over the paths from input j to output k, of the product of the local partial magnitudes along each path, R_kj = sqrt(∑_paths ∏ abs(d)^2). A 2-norm is at most the 1-norm, so R <= Utilde entrywise, with equality where an entry has a single path. If each parent slot's local partial carries an independent, equiprobable sign, R[k, j]^2 = E[J_kj^2]. This is the matrix balance_cover takes with merge = SignAveragedMerge(). Formed by one m-channel reverse sweep, O(m*L), that accumulates with hypot. No path product is squared, so an entry is accurate to rounding wherever the path products themselves are representable, as for Utilde.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.quadrature_surrogate"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.sensitivities-Union{Tuple{Tape{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"sensitivities(t::Tape) -> utilde\n\nForward absolute sensitivities: utilde[k][a] >= abs(∂v_k/∂x_a), obtained by propagating utilde[k] = sum_{i in P_k} magnitude(∂φ_k/∂v_i) * utilde[i] from utilde[x_a] = e_a. The bound follows from the triangle inequality over paths, and is tight when no path cancellation occurs. The entries are in boundtype of the tape's element type.\n\nCosts O(n * L) time and memory, so this is a diagnostic path: the sum merge is obtainable without it (see abs_reverse).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.sensitivities"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.split_fallback-Tuple{Any, Any, AbstractFloat, AbstractFloat, AbstractFloat}","page":"Internals","text":"split_fallback(lo, hi, c12, z1, z2) -> rho1\n\nThe equalizing point balanced_split aims at, for blocks where sqrt(c12*z2/z1) misses it.\n\nA pair with a ratio to read misses it only by overflow or underflow of the product under the root, and frexp recovers the point from the separated exponents: the exponent arithmetic is exact, and a rescaling by powers of two shifts e by an even amount, so this form is covariant to the bit exactly as the product form is. A pair with no ratio — a vanishing or non-finite reference — gets the geometric mean of the endpoints, the covariant, permutation-equivariant point of the feasible segment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.split_fallback"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.squared_seeds-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Tape{T}, AbstractVector}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"squared_seeds(t::Tape, rhos) -> theta2\n\nSquared seed weights: theta2[j] sums rhos[k][i]^2 over every node k and parent slot i with t.parents[k][i] == j. This is the squared-channel counterpart of the linear seeds theta that local_covers returns, and it seeds the squared channel of dependency_sweep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.squared_seeds"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.squared_surrogate-Tuple{Tape, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Internals","text":"squared_surrogate(t::Tape, outidxs::AbstractVector{<:Integer}) -> U2\n\nThe squared channel U2 = R.^2 of quadrature_surrogate: U2[k, j] sums, over the paths from input j to output k, the squared product of the local partial magnitudes along the path, so U2 <= Utilde.^2 entrywise and, under independent equiprobable signs, U2[k, j] = E[J_kj^2]. Its range is that of a squared Jacobian entry: U2[k, j] loses precision once R[k, j] falls below sqrt(floatmin(T)), and underflows to zero below about the square root of the smallest subnormal, while R itself stays accurate to rounding. The sign-averaged pair is computed on R; this matrix is for inspection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.squared_surrogate"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.tape_potentials-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Tape{T}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"tape_potentials(t::Tape, outidxs::AbstractVector{<:Integer}; root=nothing, cgiters=0)\n    -> (; theta, live, roots, nties)\n\nLog-scale potentials on the live tape graph: one number per node, least-squares-fitting the slot equations θ_child - θ_parent = log|d|. exp(θ) at the output nodes is the start balance_cover uses for start = :covariant on a tape, because rescaling a node's value by s shifts its potential by log s and leaves every difference within a component covariant.\n\nA node is live when it lies on some input-to-output path whose local derivatives are all nonzero; live is that mask over the tape's nodes, and theta is NaN outside it. The live nodes and slots form an undirected graph (parallel slots counted separately) whose components are rooted at their node of maximum undirected degree, lowest node index on ties. roots lists those node indices and nties[c] how many nodes of component c share the root's degree, so a caller can see when the root came down to the index rather than the graph. root overrides the rule with a node index or a vector of them, at most one per component and each live; components with no supplied root keep the default. A supplied root breaks no tie, so its component reports nties[c] = 1.\n\ntheta is the breadth-first layer average from each root: θ = 0 at the root, and a node in layer d takes the mean over the slots joining it to layer d-1 of θ_u ± log|d|, + when it is the slot's child. cgiters > 0 refines that with up to this many conjugate-gradient iterations on the normal equations, stopping earlier at a residual floor or at the Laplacian's rank, both of which are invariant under rescaling; past those the iteration only amplifies roundoff along the nullspace. The exact least-squares potentials are independent of the root up to one additive constant per component.\n\nCosts O(L) for cgiters = 0 and O(L) per refinement iteration, with one logarithm per live slot and no derivative evaluations. The fit weights tape slots equally, so like the surrogate itself it depends on how the program is written.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> r(x) = [2 * x[1] * x[2], 4 * x[2] * x[3]];\n\njulia> pot = DerivativeCovers.tape_potentials(tape_function(r, [1.0, 1.0, 1.0])...);\n\njulia> pot.nties, all(isnan, pot.theta[.!pot.live])\n([5], true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.tape_potentials"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.value_bounds_split-NTuple{6, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"value_bounds_split(c11, c12, c22, z1, z2, ratio) -> Bool\n\nWhether the value pair (z1, z2) fixes the split of the block [c11 c12; c12 c22].\n\nbalanced_split aims at sqrt(c12*z2/z1) and clamps it to the feasible segment [sqrt(c11), c12/sqrt(c22)], so a pair carrying no scale still yields a covariant split wherever the endpoint it runs toward is positive and finite: the clamp supplies the answer, and both endpoints are covariant. What is left is the pair of cases where that endpoint is degenerate as well — c22 == 0 puts the upper endpoint at infinity, c11 == 0 puts the lower one at zero — together with two vanishing values, which leave the ratio z2/z1 undefined whatever the block. ratio is how lopsided a pair must be to count as carrying no scale.\n\nThe test compares z1 and z2 directly, which is not invariant when the two parents carry different units, so it is applied only where no covariant split exists. In particular a block whose bounding diagonal entry is proportional to the vanishing value — a quotient's numerator, a power's exponent — has its split fixed by the values until the value is exactly zero, and that split grows without bound on the approach; see balanced_split.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.value_bounds_split"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#DerivativeCovers.worst_severity-Tuple{AbstractArray}","page":"Internals","text":"worst_severity(severity::AbstractArray) -> (s, idx)\n\nLargest entry of severity and where it occurs, treating NaN as passing.\n\nChecks over a whole array score every entry and reduce, rather than testing entries in turn and raising at the first failure: the score is an array computation and only its reduction is inspected, so the decision to raise is taken once, by the caller, on a single scalar. Each caller normalizes its severity so that entries <= 1 pass, which also makes the reported number the factor by which the worst entry misses. A NaN entry comes from a vacuous comparison — zero against zero — and scores as 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.worst_severity"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#MatrixCovers.gramcover!-Tuple{AbstractVector, Tape, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Internals","text":"gramcover!(s, t::Tape, outidxs) -> s\ngramcover!(s, t::Tape, outidxs, w::AbstractVector) -> s\ngramcover!(s, t::Tape, outidxs, W::Diagonal) -> s\ngramcover!(s, t::Tape, outidxs, W::AbstractMatrix; v=<symmetric cover of abs.(W)>) -> s\n\nWrite gramcover's cover of the Gram matrix of the tape outputs outidxs into s and return it. s must be indexed like the inputs (eachindex(s) == 1:t.ninputs).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MatrixCovers.gramcover!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#MatrixCovers.gramcover-Tuple{Tape, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Internals","text":"gramcover(t::Tape, outidxs) -> s\ngramcover(t::Tape, outidxs, w::AbstractVector) -> s\ngramcover(t::Tape, outidxs, W::Diagonal) -> s\ngramcover(t::Tape, outidxs, W::AbstractMatrix; v=<symmetric cover of abs.(W)>) -> s\n\nSymmetric cover of the Gram matrix G = J'*W*J of the Jacobian J of the tape outputs outidxs: a nonnegative vector s, one entry per input (1:t.ninputs), with abs(G[i,j]) <= s[i]*s[j] for every pair. Neither J nor G is formed. W defaults to the identity (G = J'*J, the Gauss–Newton matrix), and a vector w means W = Diagonal(w); the weights may have either sign. w, v, and both axes of W are indexed like outidxs, one entry per output.\n\nNo cover of J is needed: rescaling the outputs by a diagonal C together with W -> inv(C)*W*inv(C) leaves G unchanged, so the weight alone fixes the output scale.\n\nFor any v >= 0 with abs(W[k,l]) <= v[k]*v[l], the result is s = Ũ'*v, where Ũ >= abs.(J) is the absolute-sensitivity surrogate (jacobian_surrogate), and abs(G[i,j]) <= ∑_{k,l} Ũ[k,i]*abs(W[k,l])*Ũ[l,j] <= s[i]*s[j]. For diagonal weights v = sqrt.(abs.(w)) (all ones when unweighted), so an output with zero weight contributes nothing. For a general W the default v is MatrixCovers.symcover of max.(abs.(W), abs.(transpose(W))), which also admits an asymmetric W; a supplied v must be finite, nonnegative, and satisfy abs(W[k,l]) <= v[k]*v[l]. A repeated entry of outidxs is a repeated row of J, and its seeds add.\n\ns comes from one absolute-value reverse sweep seeded v[k] at output node outidxs[k], so it costs O(L) for a tape of length L and never forms Ũ. It sums the weighted rows of Ũ, and for w >= 0 (or no weight) it brackets the per-output quadrature h_gn[j] = sqrt(∑_k w[k]*Ũ[k,j]^2):\n\nh_gn[j] <= s[j] <= sqrt(n_j) * h_gn[j],     n_j = count(>(0), Ũ[:, j]),\n\nwhere n_j is the number of outputs that input j reaches. h_gn is the minimal symmetric cover of Ũ'*W*Ũ and costs one sweep channel per output, O(m*L). Unweighted, it is the h_gn of cover_nls with ngroups=nothing; with Ũ in hand from a WithDiagnostics result, a diagonal weight gives it as MatrixCovers.gramcover(Utilde, w).\n\nOn a tape whose sweeps round upward (see boundtype), such as one recorded over a box of intervals, the seeds and the sweep round upward, and v is raised by the few units in the last place needed for abs(W[k,l]) <= v[k]*v[l] to hold exactly, so s covers G at every point of the box. On any other tape, s carries the roundoff of the sweep without inflation, like the package's other sweep-based covers, so the bound holds to a relative error of a few eps per node on the longest path.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> r(x) = [x[1] * x[2], exp(x[1]) - x[3], x[2] + 2x[3]];\n\njulia> t, outidxs = tape_function(r, [0.5, 2.0, -1.0]);\n\njulia> round.(DerivativeCovers.gramcover(t, outidxs); digits=4)   # column sums of abs.(J)\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 3.6487\n 1.5\n 3.0\n\njulia> round.(DerivativeCovers.gramcover(t, outidxs, [1.0, 0.0, 4.0]); digits=4)   # output 2 dropped\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 2.0\n 2.5\n 4.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MatrixCovers.gramcover"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#MatrixCovers.support_components-Tuple{Tape, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Internals","text":"support_components(t::Tape, outidxs) -> sc::SupportComponents\n\nConnected components of the bipartite support graph of the Jacobian of the outputs outidxs with respect to the tape's inputs: outputs and inputs are the vertices, and each nonzero Ũ_kj is an edge joining output k to input j. The result is a MatrixCovers.SupportComponents whose row axis runs over the outputs in the order of outidxs (1:length(outidxs)) and whose column axis runs over the inputs (1:t.ninputs); query it with rowcomponent, colcomponent, and ncomponents.\n\nThis is the tape counterpart of MatrixCovers.support_components(Ũ): it costs O(L) in the tape length and never forms Ũ. A tape edge transmits sensitivity exactly where its first partial is nonzero, so the surrogate's support is carried by those edges alone, and a node is live when it both depends on an input and reaches one of outidxs along them. Any undirected path between live tape nodes splits into ascending and descending runs whose nodes each reach some output, which forces the corresponding bipartite vertices into one component; conversely a support edge is a directed path of live nodes. The undirected components of the live tape graph therefore restrict to the bipartite components exactly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MatrixCovers.support_components"},{"category":"section","location":"intervals/#Covers-over-a-box","page":"Covers over a box","text":"A cover computed at a point bounds the Hessian at that point. Some uses, such as global optimization and validated numerics, need a bound that holds over a whole region. Loading IntervalArithmetic allows the program to be recorded on a box of intervals; the covers computed from that recording hold at every point of the box:\n\njulia> using DerivativeCovers, IntervalArithmetic\n\njulia> f(x) = exp(x[1]) + x[1] * x[2]^2;\n\njulia> X = interval.([0.4, 1.0], [0.6, 1.5]);    # 0.4 ≤ x[1] ≤ 0.6, 1.0 ≤ x[2] ≤ 1.5\n\njulia> c = cover_hessian(f, X);\n\njulia> round.(c.h_sum; digits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 3.2864\n 2.6446\n\njulia> H(x) = [exp(x[1]) 2x[2]; 2x[2] 2x[1]];    # the exact Hessian, for comparison\n\njulia> all(all(abs.(H([x1, x2])) .<= c.h_sum .* c.h_sum') for x1 in 0.4:0.05:0.6, x2 in 1.0:0.1:1.5)\ntrue\n\nThe cover is a vector of floating-point numbers, while the gradient c.grad is a vector of intervals enclosing the gradient over the box.\n\nEvery function that computes a cover accepts a box, including forward_cover, cover_nls, and cover_jacobian. When the inputs are intervals, the arithmetic that accumulates the bounds rounds upward (see Upward and boundtype), so the covers hold exactly rather than up to rounding.\n\nSome operations are not defined over a box:\n\ncontrol flow that the box does not decide, such as a comparison of a variable's value when its interval contains the comparison value, raises IntervalArithmetic's InconclusiveBooleanOperation;\nan operation evaluated outside its domain on part of the box, such as log of an interval containing zero, raises a DomainError that identifies the operation;\nabs of an interval containing zero is accepted only as the base of a constant power p ≥ 2, since abs is not twice differentiable at zero;\natan(y, x) refuses a box that touches its branch cut, y = 0 with x ≤ 0, across which it is discontinuous;\nfunctions that evaluate the program at a point, such as scaled_spectral_radius with a tape, reject a tape recorded over a box.\n\nCovers of a composition F₂ ∘ F₁ can be formed from covers of the two pieces, each computed over the appropriate region; see Combining covers of pieces.","title":"Covers over a box"},{"category":"section","location":"intervals/#Supporting-other-set-valued-types","page":"Covers over a box","text":"The interval support is implemented by a small number of methods, which another set-valued number type can also implement: magnitude (the largest absolute value over the set), boundtype, decided_sign, decided_one and decided_zero, separation, and check_values.","title":"Supporting other set-valued types"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/extending/#Extension-interface","page":"Extension interface","text":"These names define how programs are recorded, how local covers are chosen, and how new operations and number types are supported. Apart from Tape, TVar, tape_function, and FVar, they are public but not exported; call them with the module prefix, for example DerivativeCovers.unarynode.\n\nPages = [\"extending.md\"]","title":"Extension interface"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/extending/#Choosing-among-local-covers","page":"Extension interface","text":"","title":"Choosing among local covers"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/extending/#Recording-programs","page":"Extension interface","text":"","title":"Recording programs"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/extending/#Adding-operations","page":"Extension interface","text":"","title":"Adding operations"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/extending/#Second-derivative-forms","page":"Extension interface","text":"","title":"Second-derivative forms"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/extending/#Sub-maps-and-composition","page":"Extension interface","text":"","title":"Sub-maps and composition"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/extending/#Set-valued-numbers","page":"Extension interface","text":"","title":"Set-valued numbers"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.split_block","page":"Extension interface","text":"split_block(ratio, c11, c12, c22, s1::SplitScale, s2::SplitScale) -> (rho1, rho2)\n\nThe member of the block's cover family the split selects, from the two channels each slot carries.\n\nParent values fix the split covariantly except at blocks where a vanishing value meets a degenerate feasible segment, and there no covariant local choice exists at all: the equalizing point runs to an endpoint that is itself zero or infinite. So the values are read wherever they carry a usable scale and the declared input scales stand in wherever they do not, which keeps covariance where it is attainable and asks for units only where it is not. value_bounds_split is the test and ratio its threshold; see split_ratio.\n\nThe guard compares two differently scaled quantities and is therefore not itself invariant: a rescaling moves a block's value ratio, and one sitting near ratio can cross it. Nothing narrower is available — the one degeneracy test a rescaling leaves alone is an exactly vanishing pair, and a value exactly zero at one point is of order the displacement at a nearby one, so that test catches a block at one evaluation point and not at its neighbors.\n\nCost: the guard is three comparisons on values already recorded, and the declared scales are built by the first block that asks for them rather than in advance. A program whose blocks all keep a usable value pair therefore pays nothing for the fallback; one that reaches a guarded block pays one extra O(L) forward sweep, once. guards_any_block answers which case a tape is in without running a cover.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.split_block"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.split_ratio","page":"Extension interface","text":"split_ratio(ratio, T::Type) -> r\n\nThe degeneracy threshold a cover over element type T runs at, from the ratio keyword its entry point was given, in M = magtype(T). nothing means sqrt(eps(M)).\n\nratio is how lopsided a value pair must be before value_bounds_split counts it as carrying no scale, and it must lie in [0, 1): at 1 every pair is condemned. It caps the split asymmetry the value channel can express, since a multiplication block aims at rho1 = sqrt(c12*z2/z1) and so at a ratio of sqrt(ratio) between the two references.\n\nThe default resolves against M rather than being stored as a constant because the quantity it bounds is representability. eps(M) is the floor below which one parent value is indistinguishable from zero at the scale of the other; sqrt(eps(M)) is the half-the-digits margin above that floor, capping the asymmetry at eps(M)^(-1/4). A constant chosen at one precision is silently wrong at another — 1e-8 sits below eps(Float32), which would collapse the guard to an exactly-vanishing test on a Float32 program, and that test does not survive an ordinary perturbation of the evaluation point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.split_ratio"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.guards_any_block","page":"Extension interface","text":"guards_any_block(t::Tape; ratio=nothing) -> Bool\n\nWhether any block of t could reach the declared-scale fallback, decided by the same value_bounds_split test the split itself applies and under the same conditions in which cover_slots! consults a scale at all.\n\nThis is how a caller learns whether the unit-scale default is load-bearing for their program. On a tape that answers false the cover reads parent values everywhere, is exactly covariant under an input rescaling, and pays no extra sweep. On one that answers true the cover imports the units its inputs are expressed in at the blocks where no covariant local choice exists, and stays covariant only when the declared scales are rescaled alongside.\n\nThe test reads recorded values and second partials, never an adjoint and never an intermediate gradient. The adjoint scales a block's three entries together, so it changes neither the value ratio nor which diagonal entries are nonzero, except where it underflows one of them to zero. Reading it that way overcounts — a block whose adjoint vanishes contributes no cover term for the fallback to reach — and, in that underflow corner alone, can undercount. Ignoring intermediate gradients overcounts too: a block with a dead_slot is covered diagonally and never consults a scale at all.\n\nSo this predicts which programs will ask for the declared scales without running a cover; the covers themselves build those scales on demand and never call it, and they are the authority on what a given tape does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.guards_any_block"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.sensitivity_scales","page":"Extension interface","text":"sensitivity_scales(w, t::Tape) -> sigma\n\nOne abs_forward sweep seeded with the declared input scales w, so sigma_j >= sum_a w[a] * abs(∂v_j/∂x_a) with equality when no path cancellation occurs. w = nothing means unit scales, which asserts that the inputs share units.\n\nThese are the references the split falls back on where a block's parent values carry no scale. They transform exactly as the values do under a rescaling of the intermediate variables, so the covariance argument of balanced_split applies to them unchanged; under an input rescaling x -> D*x the cover stays exactly covariant when the scales are rescaled alongside, w -> D*w.\n\nCost: one O(L) forward sweep, paid once per cover and only by a program that reaches the fallback.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.sensitivity_scales"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.split_scales","page":"Extension interface","text":"split_scales(w, t::Tape) -> scales\n\nThe per-node scale references local_cover_slots reads, one SplitScale per node: a lazy view of magnitude.(t.vals) for the value channel, paired with the declared scales w propagates, which are built only if some block asks for them. Obtained once per cover sweep and shared by every node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.split_scales"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.SplitScale","page":"Extension interface","text":"SplitScale(value, declared)\n\nBoth scale references split_block may read at one slot: the parent value, and the means of reaching the declared scale that stands in for it. They travel together because the choice between them belongs to a block, not to a node — a variable whose value carries scale in one product can be the vanishing operand of another — so a single scale per node cannot express the split.\n\ndeclared is whatever declared_scale resolves: the number itself where a backend already has it, and a DeferredScale where the sweep that produces it should run only if some block asks. dead_slot reads the same field, since a backend that can name a slot's declared scale can say whether the slot's intermediate gradient vanishes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.SplitScale"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.dead_slot","page":"Extension interface","text":"dead_slot(s::SplitScale) -> Bool\n\nWhether the slot's intermediate gradient ∇v is identically zero.\n\nSuch a slot contributes no outer product to the Hessian H = sum_k vbar_k * sum_{i,j} C^k_{ij} * ∇v_{p_i} * ∇v_{p_j}', so every entry of its block that involves it is multiplied away and needs no cover. cover_slots! drops those entries, which is what keeps the cover from importing a live slot's scale into a term that vanishes.\n\nThe test is exact: a tolerance would either declare a live slot dead, leaving a real Hessian entry uncovered, or compare two quantities with different units.\n\nA slot reported by a bare number carries no liveness of its own, and the number is read as the whole answer — which a declared scale is only while it cannot underflow. The backends here supply a reference that reports liveness separately: DeferredScale over a tape, ForwardScale in a propagation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.dead_slot"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.forward_scale","page":"Extension interface","text":"forward_scale(x::FVar) -> SplitScale\n\nThe scale references split_block reads at x — the forward analogue of split_scales, which reads a whole tape at once.\n\nBoth channels are already on the variable: the value, and the ForwardScale behind it. So a propagation splits its local covers by exactly the rule a tape does, and the two backends are comparable coordinate by coordinate.\n\nNothing is deferred here, unlike on a tape. sigma obeys the same recursion as the tape's sensitivity_scales sweep, sigma_k = sum_i magnitude(d_i) * sigma_{p_i}, but it is a single scalar per variable rather than a pass over recorded nodes — so carrying it costs O(1) per elemental whether or not any block asks for it, against the O(L) sweep a tape would have to run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.forward_scale"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.Tape","page":"Extension interface","text":"Tape{T}\n\nA Wengert tape recorded while a function runs on TVar inputs, holding its values and partials in the element type T. eltype(t) reports T: it is the working precision of the whole construction, and a value or weight a caller supplies is converted into it. Signed results such as gradients are returned in T; covers and other nonnegative bounds are returned in magtype(T), which is T itself for a real floating-point type, and computed in boundtype(T).\n\nT may be a type whose values stand for sets of numbers, such as an interval type from IntervalArithmetic.jl. The values and partials recorded over a box then enclose those at every point of the box, magnitude takes the largest absolute value over each set, and the covers computed from the tape hold everywhere on the box: the sweeps round their bounds upward (see boundtype), so this holds exactly rather than up to rounding. Program control flow that the set does not decide, such as a comparison between overlapping intervals, raises an error while recording.\n\nThe first ninputs nodes represent the function inputs. Later nodes are appended in topological order, so every parent precedes its child. Node k stores its value vals[k], parent indices parents[k], first partials ds[k][i] = ∂φ_k/∂v_{p_i}, and the second partials Cs[k], a Curvature holding the symmetric ∂²φ_k/∂v_{p_i}∂v_{p_j} in whatever form the elemental supplies: UnaryCurvature or BinaryCurvature for a unary or binary elemental, PairedCurvature for an inner-product node (see dotnode), DeclaredCurvature for a declared sub-map output (see submapnode), and NoCurvature for a node with no second-order term.\n\nInput nodes and constant output nodes have no parents and no second partials.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.Tape"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.TVar","page":"Extension interface","text":"TVar(t::Tape{T}, idx::Integer)\nTVar(c::Real)\nTVar{T}(c::Real)\n\nA scalar used while recording a function, carrying a value of type T. The CoverVar backend that records onto a Tape.\n\nTVar(t, idx) refers to node idx on t and takes the tape's element type. TVar(c) creates a constant with a value but no tape; this supports typed literals such as zero(eltype(x)) in generic numerical code, which reach TVar{T}(c) and so stay in the program's precision. Operations involving only constants are folded immediately. Operations that mix a constant and a recorded variable store the constant as a coefficient.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> t = Tape([0.5, 2.0]);\n\njulia> x = TVar(t, 1);\n\njulia> DerivativeCovers.isconstant(x), DerivativeCovers.value(x)\n(false, 0.5)\n\njulia> c = TVar(3.0);   # a constant: a value and no tape\n\njulia> DerivativeCovers.isconstant(c * TVar(4.0)), DerivativeCovers.value(c * TVar(4.0))\n(true, 12.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.TVar"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.tape_function","page":"Extension interface","text":"tape_function(f, x0::AbstractVector) -> (tape, outidx)      # scalar output\ntape_function(f, x0::AbstractVector) -> (tape, outidxs)     # vector output\n\nRecord f at x0 and return its Tape and output node index or indices.\n\nf must accept a vector of TVars and use operations supported by TVar. The second return value is:\n\na single TVar output: the Int index outidx of the output node;\nan AbstractVector of TVars: the Vector{Int} of output-node indices outidxs, one per entry. Input nodes and repeated outputs are preserved. A constant output receives its own node and therefore contributes a zero Jacobian row.\n\nA function whose outputs are all constant raises an error, as does a recording that check_values rejects.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> f(x) = exp(x[1]) + x[1] * x[2]^2;\n\njulia> t, outidx = tape_function(f, [0.5, 2.0]);\n\njulia> t.ninputs, outidx, t.vals[outidx]\n(2, 6, 3.648721270700128)\n\njulia> r(x) = [x[1] * x[2], exp(x[1]) - x[3], x[2] + 2x[3]];\n\njulia> tr, outidxs = tape_function(r, [0.5, 2.0, -1.0]);\n\njulia> outidxs\n3-element Vector{Int64}:\n 4\n 6\n 8\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.tape_function"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.FVar","page":"Extension interface","text":"FVar{T, S, M, SM}\n\nA scalar of type T that propagates a Hessian cover forward. This is the tape-free CoverVar backend.\n\nS and SM are the storage of the intermediate-gradient and cover channels, and M = boundtype(T) the type the cover and the declared scale are propagated in (see ForwardContext). M and SM follow from T and S, so FVar{T, S} names the variable type as well. A constant carries the storage too, because generic numerical code builds typed literals from eltype, which is all it has to reach the backend by.\n\nFor each variable, the propagation holds the value, the intermediate gradient ∇v, a squared cover of ∇²v (see ForwardChannels), the scalar declared scale sigma the split falls back on, and whether ∇v is identically zero (see ForwardScale). Working memory is O(n) per live variable rather than per recorded node, so a program whose intermediates go out of scope costs O(n × live width) and never materializes a tape.\n\nBuild one with forward_cover rather than directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.FVar"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.CoverVar","page":"Extension interface","text":"CoverVar{T}\n\nA scalar that carries a value of type T through a program so that a cover construction can observe the program's elementals as it runs. TVar records them onto a Tape.\n\nThe elementals of overloads.jl are written against this type rather than against any one backend. Each computes its value, first partials and second partials and hands them to one of four recorders, which is where the backends differ. A concrete subtype V defines:\n\nvalue and isconstant;\nconstantvar(::Type{V}, c). Generic numerical code obtains typed literals through zero, one, oneunit and convert of V, all of which route here, so a constant must carry every type parameter V has;\nwhichever of unarynode, binarynode, dotnode and linearnode it supports. An unsupported recorder makes the elementals that reach it unavailable; a subtype supporting none of them cannot run a program.\n\nA constant has no node behind it in any backend: an elemental folds a constant operand into the node that consumes it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.CoverVar"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.value","page":"Extension interface","text":"value(x)\n\nThe numeric value carried by x: the value of a CoverVar, constant or not, or x itself for a plain Real. The Real fallback lets generic code read the value of either a program variable or a plain number without a branch, detaching a quantity from the backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.value"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.isconstant","page":"Extension interface","text":"isconstant(x) -> Bool\n\nWhether x is a constant: a value with no node behind it, as returned by constantvar and by zero/one/oneunit of the element type. A constant contributes coefficients to the nodes it enters and has no node of its own.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.isconstant"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.constantvar","page":"Extension interface","text":"constantvar(::Type{V}, c::Real) -> V\n\nThe constant of backend V carrying value c and nothing else. See CoverVar.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.constantvar"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.nnodes","page":"Extension interface","text":"nnodes(t::Tape)\n\nNumber of nodes recorded on t, inputs included. This is the tape length L.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.nnodes"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.natoms","page":"Extension interface","text":"natoms(t::Tape)\n\nNumber of cover atoms recorded on t: the independent rank-1 second-order terms its nodes expose, summed over the tape (see atom_slots). A unary or binary elemental with nonzero second partials exposes one atom, an inner-product node one per parent pair, and a linear node none.\n\nThis is the group count at which cover_hybrid reaches the quadrature merge. It counts what the tape records, so a node whose adjoint or curvature vanishes at the recorded point is counted even though it seeds no channel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.natoms"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.noderef","page":"Extension interface","text":"noderef(x::TVar) -> (tape, idx)\n\nThe tape and node index behind x. A constant has neither: every elemental folds a constant operand into one that records, so reaching here with a constant means a caller bypassed that folding.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.noderef"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.pushnode!","page":"Extension interface","text":"pushnode!(t::Tape, val, parents, d, C) -> TVar\n\nAppend a node with value val, parent indices parents, first partials d, and second partials C, and return a TVar referring to it.\n\nval and d are converted to the tape's element type. C is stored as it is given, and every Curvature form the sweeps resolve statically holds that same element type, so an elemental should build its curvature in eltype(t) too.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.pushnode!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.unarynode","page":"Extension interface","text":"unarynode(x::TVar, val, d, c) -> TVar\n\nRecord a node φ(x) with value val, φ'(x) = d, and φ''(x) = c.\n\nA zero c (one whose magnitude is zero) is stored as NoCurvature rather than as a zero UnaryCurvature: the node contributes no second-order term either way, and that form says so without an entry to load and test.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.unarynode"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.binarynode","page":"Extension interface","text":"binarynode(x::TVar, y::TVar, val, d1, d2, c11, c12, c22) -> TVar\n\nRecord a node φ(x, y) with value val, first partials (d1, d2), and second partials [c11 c12; c12 c22].\n\nAn all-zero block is stored as NoCurvature, for the reason unarynode stores a zero c that way. Addition and subtraction of two tape variables are the common case.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.binarynode"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.dotnode","page":"Extension interface","text":"dotnode(ws::AbstractVector{<:TVar}, xs::AbstractVector{<:TVar}) -> TVar\n\nRecord an inner-product node y = sum_j w_j*x_j. Parent slots are ordered (w_1, ..., w_q, x_1, ..., x_q), first partials are (x_1, ..., x_q, w_1, ..., w_q), and the second-partial matrix is PairedCurvature(q). Parent slots are independent even when ws and xs share entries (e.g. dotnode(xs, xs)): the decomposition is over slots, not values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.dotnode"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.linearnode","page":"Extension interface","text":"linearnode(coeffs::AbstractVector{<:Real}, xs::AbstractVector{<:TVar}) -> TVar\n\nRecord a node y = sum_j coeffs[j]*xs[j] for constant coeffs. First partials are the coefficients converted to the tape's element type; the second-partial matrix is empty, since a linear combination of constants and TVars has no second-order term.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.linearnode"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.constantnode","page":"Extension interface","text":"constantnode(t::Tape, c::Real) -> TVar\n\nRecord a node with value c, no parents, and empty partials. Its absolute sensitivity is zero, so its Jacobian row vanishes and it contributes no atom to any cover.\n\nElementals fold a constant operand into the node that consumes it rather than giving it a node (see TVar), so this is reached only at the boundary where a program's outputs become node indices and a constant needs an index to be one: a fixed offset among a program's outputs, such as the unit residual that carries an additive constant of a least-squares loss.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.constantnode"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.atom_slots","page":"Extension interface","text":"atom_slots(C::Curvature, nslots) -> atoms\n\nThe groups of a node's parent slots that the quadrature merge of cover_hessian combines separately. One atom spanning every slot is correct whenever the node's cover is a single rank-1 term. A PairedCurvature(q) instead forms q atoms, one per pair (j, q+j), matching the block-diagonal structure of its second partials: the atoms it contributes are independent rank-1 terms, not one combined term for the whole node. A NoCurvature forms none.\n\nThe atoms must partition the slots carrying cover weight: a slot left out of every atom drops its contribution from the quadrature merge and from cover_hybrid, and a slot named twice counts it twice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.atom_slots"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.check_finite_partials","page":"Extension interface","text":"check_finite_partials(site::CoverSite, av, c::Tuple)\n\nThrow unless the adjoint weight av of the elemental at site and its covering entries c are all finite, reporting the values around it.\n\nThe covering invariant is a set of comparisons against c, so it cannot fire meaningfully when c is not finite: every comparison against NaN is false and no finite rho covers an infinite entry, so the test would reject a split that is not at fault. A non-finite c says instead that the program is not twice-differentiable where it was evaluated — a division by zero, or a power, logarithm or root at its singularity. A non-finite av says the same of a node downstream, whose partials the adjoint has already passed through.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.check_finite_partials"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.check_values","page":"Extension interface","text":"check_values(t::Tape)\ncheck_values(site::CoverSite, val, ds, cs)\n\nThrow if the values recorded on t show that the program left the domain of one of its elementals. tape_function calls this once recording finishes. Over numbers there is nothing to check, since a numeric elemental outside its domain raises an error or returns a non-finite value the cover checks report. A set-valued element type, whose elementals can instead return a result flagged as undefined on part of the set, extends this to look for that flag.\n\nThe second form checks one elemental: its value val, its first partials ds and its second partials cs, with site naming it in the error. A propagation that keeps no tape, such as forward_cover, calls it at each elemental.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.check_values"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.Curvature","page":"Extension interface","text":"Curvature\n\nThe second partials ∂²φ_k/∂v_{p_i}∂v_{p_j} of one tape node, in whatever form that node's elemental can supply them. Tape stores one per node.\n\nCover constructions never need the entries themselves, only bounds on their magnitudes, so a curvature may declare a bound instead of recording a matrix (DeclaredCurvature). The sweeps reach every form through the methods below, which is what lets a new elemental introduce a new form without touching them.\n\nA new subtype must define:\n\ncover_slots: the node's rank-1 cover, slot-aligned with its parents. It reads its scale references and its error context through a CoverSite rather than off a tape, so one definition serves every backend.\nbound_contribution!: the node's term of the elementwise bound B.\natom_slots: the parent-slot groups the quadrature merge combines separately. The fallback treats every slot as one atom, which is correct for any curvature whose cover is a single rank-1 term.\n\nA subtype whose entries are recorded exactly may also define:\n\ncurvature_apply!: the second-order term of a Hessian-vector product.\nhas_exact_partials: false suppresses hvp on any tape containing the node. The fallback is true, so a subtype that declares a bound must override it.\n\ngetindex and size are introspection, not part of the contract: a curvature that records no entries has none to report.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.Curvature"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.NoCurvature","page":"Extension interface","text":"NoCurvature() <: Curvature\n\nThe curvature of a node with no second-order term: an input node, a linear n-ary node such as linearnode, or an elemental whose curvature at the recorded point is zero. It contributes to no cover and to no bound, so the sweeps skip it by dispatch rather than by inspecting a matrix of zeros.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.NoCurvature"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.UnaryCurvature","page":"Extension interface","text":"UnaryCurvature(c) <: Curvature\n\nThe second partial φ''(x) of a unary elemental, held inline as the single number it is.\n\nA tape stores its curvatures in a container with abstract element type, so every form is reached through a reference; holding the entry inline rather than in a 1×1 matrix saves the second reference on the way to it, once per node per sweep, and an allocation per node while recording.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.UnaryCurvature"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.BinaryCurvature","page":"Extension interface","text":"BinaryCurvature(c11, c12, c22) <: Curvature\n\nThe second partials [c11 c12; c12 c22] of a binary elemental, slot-aligned with the node's two parents and held inline. Symmetry is structural: one entry stands for both off-diagonal slots.\n\nInline for the reason UnaryCurvature is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.BinaryCurvature"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.DenseCurvature","page":"Extension interface","text":"DenseCurvature(C::AbstractMatrix) <: Curvature\n\nRecorded second partials as an explicit symmetric matrix, slot-aligned with the node's parents.\n\nThis is the general form, for a curvature with no structure to exploit and no fixed arity. The elementals of overloads.jl do not use it: a unary or binary block is UnaryCurvature or BinaryCurvature, which hold their entries inline.\n\nThe rank-1 cover of a block larger than 2×2 is not a single split, so cover_slots rejects one; the elementwise bound and the Hessian-vector product read any arity. An elemental with more parents whose curvature does have structure supplies a form that carries it, as PairedCurvature and DeclaredCurvature do.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.DenseCurvature"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.PairedCurvature","page":"Extension interface","text":"PairedCurvature(q::Int) <: Curvature\n\nCurvature of an inner-product node with q pairs of parent slots ordered (w_1, ..., w_q, x_1, ..., x_q) (see dotnode). Entry (i, j) is 1 when i and j are paired (abs(i - j) == q), the sole nonzero second partials ∂²y/∂w_j∂x_j of y = sum_j w_j*x_j, and 0 otherwise.\n\nThe 2q × 2q matrix is never materialized: it has 2q nonzeros, and its block-diagonal structure lets every operation run over the q pairs directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.PairedCurvature"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.DeclaredCurvature","page":"Extension interface","text":"DeclaredCurvature(s::AbstractVector) <: Curvature\n\nDeclared bound on the second partials of a sub-map output (see submapnode): abs(C[i, j]) <= s[i]*s[j], with the true C not recorded. s is nonnegative and slot-aligned with the node's parents. On a Tape{T}, s is a vector of magtype(T).\n\nCover constructions consume second partials only through bounds on abs.(C), so the declaration substitutes for a recorded matrix everywhere except hvp, which needs the signed entries and fails fast — hence has_exact_partials is false. Covers built over such a node are valid conditional on the declaration, which is the caller's assertion: nothing on the tape can verify it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.DeclaredCurvature"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.cover_slots","page":"Extension interface","text":"cover_slots(C::Curvature, ratio, site, av, sl; check=true) -> Union{Nothing,Vector{M}}\ncover_slots!(rho::Vector, C::Curvature, ratio, site, av, sl; check=true) -> Bool\n\nThe rank-1 cover of one elemental, slot-aligned with its operands: nonnegative rho with rho[i]*rho[j] >= av*magnitude(C[i,j]), where av is the weight the elemental's second-order term carries (the absolute adjoint magnitude(vbar[k]) in a reverse sweep, 1 in a forward one). Returns nothing when the elemental contributes no second-order term.\n\nEntries involving a slot whose intermediate gradient vanishes are exempt: they are multiplied by zero in the Hessian, so covering them would only import the other slot's scale into a term that is not there. dead_slot is the test, and it reads the slot's entry of sl.\n\nrho, av and the entries of sl are magnitudes, in M = magtype(eltype(site)). rho must be a Vector{M}: local_cover_slots asserts that type so that the sweeps, which call it once per node, need not box the result. The entries av*magnitude(C[i,j]) and the products that cover them are formed in boundtype(eltype(site)), so where that rounds upward the invariant holds exactly rather than up to rounding.\n\nThe mutating form resizes rho to the elemental's slot count and fills it, returning whether there is a second-order term at all and leaving rho untouched when there is not. Every construction lives there, so that a sweep can hand it one buffer and let no node allocate for its slot weights; cover_slots allocates a fresh rho and forwards.\n\nsplit_block breaks the tie at off-diagonal-active blocks; ratio is its degeneracy threshold and sl its scale references, one per slot and in slot order — not indexed by node, so that a backend holding no nodes can supply them. check verifies the covering invariant against the recorded entries; a curvature that declares a bound rather than recording entries has nothing to check against.\n\nsite is a CoverSite, used for the element type and for error reporting. This is the per-elemental kernel shared by local_covers, the CoverOnly sweep, and the forward propagation; see local_cover_slots.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.cover_slots"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.cover_slots!","page":"Extension interface","text":"cover_slots(C::Curvature, ratio, site, av, sl; check=true) -> Union{Nothing,Vector{M}}\ncover_slots!(rho::Vector, C::Curvature, ratio, site, av, sl; check=true) -> Bool\n\nThe rank-1 cover of one elemental, slot-aligned with its operands: nonnegative rho with rho[i]*rho[j] >= av*magnitude(C[i,j]), where av is the weight the elemental's second-order term carries (the absolute adjoint magnitude(vbar[k]) in a reverse sweep, 1 in a forward one). Returns nothing when the elemental contributes no second-order term.\n\nEntries involving a slot whose intermediate gradient vanishes are exempt: they are multiplied by zero in the Hessian, so covering them would only import the other slot's scale into a term that is not there. dead_slot is the test, and it reads the slot's entry of sl.\n\nrho, av and the entries of sl are magnitudes, in M = magtype(eltype(site)). rho must be a Vector{M}: local_cover_slots asserts that type so that the sweeps, which call it once per node, need not box the result. The entries av*magnitude(C[i,j]) and the products that cover them are formed in boundtype(eltype(site)), so where that rounds upward the invariant holds exactly rather than up to rounding.\n\nThe mutating form resizes rho to the elemental's slot count and fills it, returning whether there is a second-order term at all and leaving rho untouched when there is not. Every construction lives there, so that a sweep can hand it one buffer and let no node allocate for its slot weights; cover_slots allocates a fresh rho and forwards.\n\nsplit_block breaks the tie at off-diagonal-active blocks; ratio is its degeneracy threshold and sl its scale references, one per slot and in slot order — not indexed by node, so that a backend holding no nodes can supply them. check verifies the covering invariant against the recorded entries; a curvature that declares a bound rather than recording entries has nothing to check against.\n\nsite is a CoverSite, used for the element type and for error reporting. This is the per-elemental kernel shared by local_covers, the CoverOnly sweep, and the forward propagation; see local_cover_slots.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.cover_slots!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.bound_contribution!","page":"Extension interface","text":"bound_contribution!(B, C::Curvature, av, ps, utilde)\n\nAccumulate node k's term of the elementwise bound into B: av * sum_{i,j} magnitude(C[i,j]) * utilde[ps[i]] * utilde[ps[j]]', where av is the absolute adjoint and utilde the forward absolute sensitivities. See hessian_bound.\n\nA DeclaredCurvature contributes its declared bound s[i]*s[j] >= abs(C[i,j]), which factorizes, so its term is the single rank-1 outer product w*w' with w = sum_i s[i]*utilde[ps[i]].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.bound_contribution!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.curvature_apply!","page":"Extension interface","text":"curvature_apply!(vbardot, C::Curvature, ps, bk, dv)\n\nAccumulate the second-order term of a Hessian-vector product into vbardot: vbardot[ps[i]] += bk * sum_j C[i,j] * dv[ps[j]], where bk is the node's adjoint and dv the forward tangents. See hvp.\n\nDefined only for a curvature that records its entries exactly; has_exact_partials reports which do.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.curvature_apply!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.has_exact_partials","page":"Extension interface","text":"has_exact_partials(C::Curvature) -> Bool\n\nWhether C records its second partials exactly, so an exact second-order product can be formed through it. hvp refuses to run on a tape carrying any node that answers false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.has_exact_partials"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.CoverSite","page":"Extension interface","text":"CoverSite\n\nWhere a local cover is being built, carried by cover_slots for error reporting.\n\nThe split needs the element type to work in and enough context to say which elemental it failed on, and nothing else about the backend. Naming that little through a site is what lets one split serve a tape sweep and a tapeless forward pass alike.\n\nA subtype defines Base.eltype, site_label and site_values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.CoverSite"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.TapeSite","page":"Extension interface","text":"TapeSite(t::Tape, k::Integer)\n\nThe CoverSite naming node k of t, so that a cover built there reports which node it failed on and what the values around it were.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.TapeSite"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.site_label","page":"Extension interface","text":"site_label(site::CoverSite) -> AbstractString\n\nHow to name the elemental in an error message, such as \"node 7\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.site_label"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.site_values","page":"Extension interface","text":"site_values(site::CoverSite) -> AbstractString\n\nThe values surrounding the elemental, for the error that reports where a program stopped being twice-differentiable: \"node value 1.5, parent values (0.3, 2.0)\" and the like.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.site_values"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.submap","page":"Extension interface","text":"submap(F, xs::AbstractVector{<:CoverVar}; scales=nothing, ratio=nothing) -> Vector{<:CoverVar}\n\nRecord F as a single declared call on the tape of xs: evaluate declare_submap at the parent values and embed the result via submapnode. The recording tape gains one node per output of F — independent of how many operations F performs internally — at the price that exact Hessian-vector products (hvp) become unavailable on that tape. A scalar-valued F yields a one-element vector. scales and ratio are passed to declare_submap and govern only the declaration's curvature covers.\n\nThe declaration is recorded on the parent values themselves, so on a tape whose values stand for sets, such as intervals over a box, it holds over the same sets as the rest of the tape.\n\nThe declaration it embeds is dense in the sub-map's outputs and arguments, so this keeps the outer tape on one tape at diagnostic tier. compose_jacobian_cover and compose_hessian_cover cover the same composite from covers of the factors alone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.submap"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.declare_submap","page":"Extension interface","text":"declare_submap(F, x0::AbstractVector; scales=nothing, ratio=nothing) -> (; y, J, S)\n\nThe declaration submapnode consumes, distilled from F's own scratch tape at x0: exact values y = F(x0), the exact signed Jacobian J from per-output reverse sweeps, and curvature covers S whose row k is the sum-merge cover of ∇²F_k, so abs.(∇²F_k) <= S[k, :]*S[k, :]' by cover_hessian's guarantee. F may return a single TVar (declared as one output) or a vector of them; a linear output gets an exactly zero row of S.\n\nCosts O(q*L_F) time — one q-channel adjoint sweep over F's tape plus one absolute sweep per output — once per declaration, and O(q*n) memory for J and S, which places it at diagnostic tier alongside submapnode; the scratch tape is discarded afterwards. The declaration transforms covariantly under rescalings of F's arguments and outputs because each ingredient inherits the covariance of the underlying sweeps, including the zero-parent-value fallback exception of the local tie-break.\n\nscales and ratio govern the split behind the rows of S, as in cover_hessian. The scratch tape's inputs are F's arguments, so scales is expressed in those units, not in the recording tape's. The two splits are otherwise independent: the recording tape consumes S as a fixed bound, so its covers stay valid whatever choice stands behind the declaration.\n\ny and J are in the element type of x0 (as Tape assigns it) and S in its magtype. The declaration holds wherever x0 does: at a point for numeric x0, and at every point of the box for an interval x0, whose y and J then enclose the values and Jacobian over the box and whose S covers the curvature there. A tape whose values stand for sets accepts only a declaration of the second kind (see submapnode).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.declare_submap"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.submapnode","page":"Extension interface","text":"submapnode(xs::AbstractVector{<:TVar}, y::AbstractVector{<:Real}, J::AbstractMatrix{<:Real},\n           S::Union{Nothing,AbstractMatrix{<:Real}}=nothing) -> Vector{TVar}\n\nRecord a declared sub-map call y = F(v) as one node per output, without retracing F's internals: node k carries the value y[k], the arguments xs as parents, the exact signed Jacobian row J[k, :] (J[k, i] = ∂F_k/∂v_i at the parent values) as its first partials, and the declared curvature cover S[k, :] as a DeclaredCurvature: abs(∂²F_k/∂v_i∂v_j) <= S[k, i]*S[k, j]. S = nothing, or an all-zero row of S, declares that output linear in the arguments (NoCurvature).\n\nGradients and every first-order sweep through the call are exact. Hessian covers are valid conditional on the declaration, which is the caller's assertion — nothing on the tape can verify it — and exact Hessian-vector products are unavailable, so hvp throws on any tape containing a declared call. For the covers to stay scale-covariant the declaration must transform as y -> C*y, J -> C*J*inv(D), S -> sqrt.(C)*S*inv(D) under a rescaling v -> D*v of the arguments and a rescaling y -> C*y of the sub-map outputs (a symmetric cover scales as the square root of its output's scale); declare_submap builds declarations with both properties. The covers are then invariant to the output gauge C, which cancels between each output's adjoint and its declared curvature.\n\nBoth matrices are dense and are held per node, so a declared call costs O(q*p) memory and declare_submap one adjoint sweep per output to build: diagnostic tier. Where neither is affordable, compose_jacobian_cover and compose_hessian_cover combine covers of the two factors with no joint tape and no dense J. That route bounds the composition's first-order contribution by a rank-1 pair rather than using J itself, which loosens the J'∇²g J term of the chain rule and leaves the declared curvature term unchanged.\n\nOn a tape whose element type stands for sets (its magtype differs from it), y and J must be of such a type too, enclosing the values and Jacobian over the sets the parents range over, and S must cover the curvature over them; a declaration with a pointwise y or J is an ArgumentError. submap meets this by declaring on the parent values themselves.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.submapnode"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.compose_jacobian_cover","page":"Extension interface","text":"compose_jacobian_cover(a2, b2, a1, b1) -> (; a, b)\n\nCover of a product of covered factors: if abs.(J2) <= a2*b2' (m × q) and abs.(J1) <= a1*b1' (q × n), then abs.(J2*J1) <= (b2⋅a1) * a2*b1', so the returned pair a = sqrt(b2⋅a1)*a2, b = sqrt(b2⋅a1)*b1 covers J2*J1 — the Jacobian of a composition F2 ∘ F1 wherever each factor's cover holds at the matching point. For the composite cover to hold over a set of points x, such as a box, (a1, b1) must hold at every x in the set and (a2, b2) at every F1(x). The inner product b2⋅a1 is invariant under rescalings of the input, middle, and output spaces (b2 and a1 carry inverse middle-space units), and its square root is split onto both sides so the rule commutes with a rescaling of either factor.\n\nEquality holds entrywise for sign-definite rank-1 factors covered exactly; in general the rule pays the same signed-cancellation looseness as any magnitude-level bound on a product.\n\nThe arithmetic is that of the inputs' element type. Pass them as Upward vectors for a composite that holds exactly rather than up to rounding, as covers over a box should.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.compose_jacobian_cover"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.compose_hessian_cover","page":"Extension interface","text":"compose_hessian_cover(hg, gamma, a, b, S=nothing) -> (; h_sum, h_quad)\n\nSymmetric covers of the Hessian of a composite f = g ∘ F at x, with y = F(x), from covers of the pieces:\n\nhg: symmetric cover of ∇²g(y) (abs.(∇²g) <= hg*hg'), one entry per output of F;\ngamma: entrywise bound gamma[k] >= abs(∂g/∂y_k) at y;\n(a, b): Jacobian cover of F at x (abs.(J_F) <= a*b');\nS: curvature covers with abs.(∇²F_k) <= S[k, :]*S[k, :]' (as declare_submap returns); nothing declares F linear.\n\nThe result covers ∇²f at every x where all four inputs hold. At a single point, exact gradient magnitudes qualify as gamma. For a cover over a set of points, such as a box, each input must hold over the whole set: (a, b) and S at every x in it, and hg and gamma at every F(x); the magnitudes of an interval gradient enclosing ∂g/∂y over the set qualify as gamma. The arithmetic is that of the inputs' element type; pass them as Upward vectors for a composite that holds exactly rather than up to rounding, as covers over a box should.\n\nThe chain rule ∇²f = J_F'*∇²g*J_F + Σ_k (∂g/∂y_k)*∇²F_k bounds entrywise as abs.(∇²f) <= (a⋅hg)² * b*b' + Σ_k gamma[k] * S[k,:]*S[k,:]' — a sum of rank-1 magnitude atoms u_0 = (a⋅hg)*b and u_k = sqrt(gamma[k])*S[k,:]. Both merges are returned: the sum merge h_sum = Σ_α u_α (cross terms are nonnegative slack) and the quadrature merge h_quad[i] = sqrt(Σ_α u_α[i]²) (Cauchy–Schwarz), with h_quad <= h_sum coordinatewise.\n\nFor g = 0.5*norm(r)^2 this generic rule gives norm(a, 1)*b for the Gauss–Newton term: the sum-merge Gram cover with each row of the surrogate Ũ replaced by its rank-1 bound a[k]*b. It is therefore at least the tape's gramcover(t, outidxs) = Ũ'*ones(m), which is the default h_gn of cover_nls and in turn at least its per-residual quadrature (ngroups=nothing); NLS consumers should prefer cover_nls.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.compose_hessian_cover"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.magnitude","page":"Extension interface","text":"magnitude(x)\n\nThe largest absolute value x can take: the quantity the cover sweeps accumulate wherever they need the size of a value, partial or adjoint. For a number this is abs(x).\n\nA type whose values stand for a set of numbers, such as an interval, extends this with the largest absolute value over the set. A program recorded on such values then yields covers that hold at every point of the set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.magnitude"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.magtype","page":"Extension interface","text":"magtype(T)\n\nThe type of magnitude applied to a T, which is the type the cover sweeps compute in for a Tape{T}. It is T for a real floating-point type and the real type of a complex one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.magtype"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.boundtype","page":"Extension interface","text":"boundtype(T)\n\nThe type the cover sweeps over a Tape{T} accumulate their bounds in. For a type whose values are points this is magtype(T), and the covers hold up to the rounding of the sweeps. A type whose values stand for sets, such as an interval, opts in to rigorous covers by returning an Upward type, so that every sum, product and square root the sweeps take rounds upward and the covers hold for every point of the set. The covers are returned in magtype(T) either way.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.boundtype"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.Upward","page":"Extension interface","text":"Upward{F}(x)\n\nA nonnegative magnitude of floating-point type F whose arithmetic rounds upward: +, *, /, sqrt, hypot and integer powers return a value no smaller than the exact result. Each elementary operation rounds to nearest and then steps to nextfloat, except where the result is exact (an operand that is zero, or a factor or divisor that is one); x <= nextfloat(fl(x)) holds for any rounding to nearest, so this needs no control over the hardware rounding mode. hypot and the powers compose these operations, so they may step more than once.\n\nA cover is a bound built from magnitudes by sums, products and square roots, each increasing in its operands, so rounding every step upward makes the computed cover at least the exact one. Subtraction is not increasing in its second operand and throws, and transcendental functions are not defined; a computation that reaches one fails rather than silently losing the bound. Division rounds upward too, and is an upper bound only where its divisor is fixed rather than computed, such as a chosen weight.\n\nMixing an Upward with a plain real promotes to Upward, so an operation rounds upward whenever either operand is one. Converting an Upward{F} to F is exact.\n\nboundtype selects this type for the sweeps over a tape whose values stand for sets.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.Upward"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.decided_sign","page":"Extension interface","text":"decided_sign(x)\n\n1 if x is positive, -1 if it is negative, and 0 otherwise: at zero, and for a value whose sign cannot be determined, such as NaN. A type whose values stand for sets extends this to return 1 or -1 only when every member of the set has that sign, so that a set containing zero, or members of both signs, gives 0.\n\nabs of a program variable uses this to decide whether the kink of abs at zero is reached.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.decided_sign"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.decided_one","page":"Extension interface","text":"decided_one(c)\ndecided_zero(c)\n\nWhether the constant c is one (resp. zero): isone(c) and iszero(c) for a number. A type whose values stand for sets extends these to return true only for the set containing just that number, and false, never an error, when the comparison cannot be decided.\n\nOperations with a constant use these to skip work an identity makes unnecessary, such as recording a node for x * c with a unit c, or the second partial of x^c with c zero or one. A false answer keeps the general form, which is always valid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.decided_one"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/extending/#DerivativeCovers.separation","page":"Extension interface","text":"separation(x, y)\n\nHow far apart x and y are: abs(x - y) for numbers. A type whose values stand for sets extends this with the smallest distance between members of the two sets, which is zero whenever they overlap. The consistency checks of cover_hessian use it to compare two computations of the same gradient.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.separation"},{"category":"section","location":"covariance/#Scale-covariance","page":"Scale covariance","text":"A cover is useful as a set of natural scales only if it does not depend on the units in which the inputs are expressed. This page states when the covers computed by this package have this property.","title":"Scale covariance"},{"category":"section","location":"covariance/#Covariance","page":"Scale covariance","text":"Rescaling the inputs, x → D*x for a positive diagonal D, changes the Hessian to inv(D)*H*inv(D). Rescaling the output, f → c*f with c > 0, changes it to c*H. A Hessian cover is covariant if it transforms the same way: h → inv(D)*h and h → sqrt(c)*h. For a Jacobian cover, rescaling the inputs by D and the outputs by C should give b → inv(D)*b and a → C*a, up to the scalar factor that a Jacobian cover leaves free.\n\nIn the following example, the program g is f with its inputs expressed in different units:\n\njulia> using DerivativeCovers\n\njulia> f(x) = x[1] * x[2] + exp(x[2]);\n\njulia> x0 = [2.0, 0.5];\n\njulia> D = [10.0, 0.1];\n\njulia> g(y) = f(y ./ D);\n\njulia> cover_hessian(g, D .* x0).h_sum ≈ cover_hessian(f, x0).h_sum ./ D\ntrue\n\njulia> cover_hessian(x -> 9 * f(x), x0).h_sum ≈ 3 * cover_hessian(f, x0).h_sum\ntrue\n\nThe covers of this package are covariant on any program in which every operation can choose its local cover from the values of its arguments. The rest of this page explains what this means and what happens otherwise.","title":"Covariance"},{"category":"section","location":"covariance/#Choosing-among-equally-valid-local-covers","page":"Scale covariance","text":"The product x * y has second partial derivatives [0 1; 1 0]. Any pair (ρ₁, ρ₂) with ρ₁ * ρ₂ ≥ 1 covers this matrix, so the operation has a one-parameter family of equally valid local covers, and the choice determines how its contribution is distributed between x and y. A choice based on a fixed number, such as ρ₁ = ρ₂ = 1, would not be covariant: if x is measured in millimeters instead of meters, its share of the cover should change by a factor of 1000.\n\nThe package resolves the choice using the magnitudes of the arguments. For a product, it chooses ρ₁ = sqrt(|y| / |x|) and ρ₂ = sqrt(|x| / |y|), which transform correctly under any rescaling of x and y. In the example above, x0 = [2.0, 0.5] gives ρ₁ = 0.5 and ρ₂ = 2; the second entry of the cover adds sqrt(exp(0.5)) ≈ 1.284 for the exp term:\n\njulia> cover_hessian(f, x0).h_sum\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.5\n 3.2840254166877414\n\nOther operations with a family of local covers, such as division and powers with a variable exponent, are resolved in the same way. This choice is made by split_block.","title":"Choosing among equally valid local covers"},{"category":"section","location":"covariance/#When-argument-values-carry-no-scale","page":"Scale covariance","text":"The rule above fails when an argument is zero: ρ₂ = sqrt(|x| / |y|) is infinite at y = 0, and no choice based on the argument values is both finite and covariant. At such operations the package uses declared scales for the inputs instead. The scales keyword, accepted by every cover function, supplies one positive scale per input; the default, nothing, sets them all to 1, which amounts to asserting that the inputs share units. guards_any_block reports whether a recorded program contains such an operation:\n\njulia> x1 = [2.0, 0.0];\n\njulia> t, out = tape_function(f, x1);\n\njulia> DerivativeCovers.guards_any_block(t)\ntrue\n\njulia> cover_hessian(f, x1).h_sum\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 1.0\n 2.0\n\njulia> cover_hessian(f, x1; scales=[1.0, 100.0]).h_sum\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 10.0\n  1.1\n\nThe declared scales affect only which valid cover is chosen, never validity. When the inputs are rescaled, a cover that depends on declared scales is covariant only if the scales are rescaled along with them, scales → D*scales:\n\njulia> cover_hessian(g, D .* x1; scales=D).h_sum .* D\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 1.0\n 2.0\n\nOn a program for which guards_any_block returns false, the scales keyword has no effect and the cover is covariant without it.\n\nThe test for \"carries no scale\" is not limited to exact zeros. An argument pair whose magnitudes differ by more than a factor ratio is treated the same way, so that a value that is zero only up to roundoff does not lead to an enormous local cover. The ratio keyword sets this threshold; the default, sqrt(eps()) for Float64, is described in split_ratio. Because this test compares two values that may have different units, a rescaling can move an operation across the threshold, and covariance then holds only approximately.\n\nNear such points the cover can be discontinuous. For a quotient x / y, as x approaches zero the local cover remains covariant but grows without bound; at x = 0 it drops to the finite cover given by the declared scales.","title":"When argument values carry no scale"},{"category":"section","location":"covariance/#Jacobian-covers","page":"Scale covariance","text":"The default start=:covariant of cover_jacobian produces products a[k] * b[j] that transform exactly under rescaling of the inputs, the outputs, and the intermediate values of the program. The :equal start instead treats all outputs alike: permuting the outputs permutes the cover, but the products do not transform exactly under rescaling.","title":"Jacobian covers"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"Test problems for studying cover tightness, from the companion package DerivativeCoverProblems.\n\nPages = [\"problems.md\"]","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems"},{"category":"module","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.DerivativeCoverProblems","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"DerivativeCoverProblems\n\nParameterized families of scalar programs for studying cover quality.\n\nThe programs are written in generic Julia and depend on nothing here, so any operator-overloading tool can run them; DerivativeCovers tapes them by virtue of DerivativeCovers.TVar overloading the elementals they use.\n\nEach generator returns a CoverProblem: a program f written from those elementals, the point x0 to evaluate it at, an optional idiomatic-Julia twin that computes the same function by an independent route, and an optional analytic hessian. The families differ in the property that drives cover looseness — how much the paths from an input to the output reconverge — and each has knobs for size, reconvergence, and dynamic range:\n\nquadratic: x'Ax/2 with A = W*W' of prescribed rank, written entrywise, as a matrix-vector product, or in factored form. The same A in three programs, so the three differ only in graph structure.\ndeep_composition: layers of width channels around a shared trunk, so each layer fans a single node out to width consumers that immediately reconverge.\nrandom_dag: a random program whose sharing parameter interpolates from a tree (sharing = 0) to heavy subexpression reuse (sharing = 1).\nadjoint_cancellation: a chain of multiplication nodes the output is nearly stationary in, so the paths from each node to the output cancel in the adjoints rather than the intermediate gradients. converged_inner_solve reaches the same condition by the envelope theorem.\n\nWide dynamic range enters along two independent axes: sigma widens the log-magnitude spread of a family's coefficients, while rescale (with scales from logscales) spreads the units of the inputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.DerivativeCoverProblems"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.CoverProblem","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"CoverProblem\n\nA scalar program together with the references needed to judge a cover of its Hessian.\n\nname: a short identifier, unique within a family sweep.\nf: the program. Built only from the elementals overloaded for DerivativeCovers.TVar, so f(x) records a tape when x is a vector of TVars, and evaluates normally on any other numeric vector (Float64, ForwardDiff.Dual, ...).\nx0: the point at which the Hessian is covered.\ntwin: an idiomatic-Julia implementation of the same function, or nothing. It is written independently of f, by an independent route — array operations f cannot express (most families), or an explicit scalar loop when f is itself written in array notation (see mlp) — so agreement between the two is evidence that f is the function it claims to be.\nhessian: x -> H, the analytic Hessian, or nothing when no closed form is available. Use exact_hessian rather than the field.\nxstar: a global minimizer of f, or nothing when none is known. Problems built with a planted solution carry it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.CoverProblem"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.DagRecipe","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"A random program, stored as the recipe that generated it: ops[k] builds node ninputs + k from earlier nodes, and the output is the sum of outputs. Replaying the recipe on a vector of any numeric type evaluates the same function, so one recipe serves the tape, the reference derivatives, and the plain evaluation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.DagRecipe"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.JacobianProblem","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"JacobianProblem\n\nA vector-valued program together with the references needed to judge an asymmetric cover of its Jacobian — the vector-output counterpart of CoverProblem.\n\nname: a short identifier, unique within a family sweep.\nr: the program, mapping an input vector to an output vector. Built only from the elementals overloaded for DerivativeCovers.TVar, so r(x) records a tape when x is a vector of TVars and evaluates normally on any other numeric vector.\nx0: the point at which the Jacobian is covered.\nnout: the number of outputs of r.\ntwin: an independent idiomatic-Julia implementation of the same map, or nothing.\njacobian: x -> J, the analytic nout × n Jacobian, or nothing when no closed form is available. Use exact_jacobian rather than the field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.JacobianProblem"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems._coeffs-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, Vararg{Integer}}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"Coefficients with magnitude abs(randn()) * exp(sigma * randn()): unit scale at sigma = 0, and log-magnitudes spread by sigma beyond that.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems._coeffs"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems._lincomb-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}, Any}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"The linear combination sum_i c[i] * z[i], recorded one term at a time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems._lincomb"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems._rowdot-Tuple{Any, Any, Integer}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"The left-to-right chain w[1]*z[1] + w[2]*z[2] + ... + w[n]*z[n], the scalar order a dot node's parents follow.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems._rowdot"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems._sum-Tuple{Any}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"Sum the entries of v using only the binary + of its element type. v must be non-empty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems._sum"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems._take_parent!-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, Vector{Int64}, Int64, Real}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"Take a parent for a new node. With probability sharing (or when nothing is unconsumed) reuse an arbitrary existing node, giving it a second consumer and so creating reconvergence; otherwise consume a node from frontier, which leaves it with exactly one consumer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems._take_parent!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.adjoint_cancellation-Tuple{Integer}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"adjoint_cancellation(n; delta=1e-2, sigma=0.0, rng=MersenneTwister(1)) -> CoverProblem\n\nA chain of n - 1 multiplication nodes u_j = x_j * x_{j+1}, each reaching the output through u_j - sin(u_j), evaluated where every u_j is small.\n\nu - sin(u) has derivative 1 - cos(u), which vanishes to second order at u = 0, so the output is nearly stationary in each u_j: the signed adjoint of that node is ~u_j^2/2 while its two paths to the output contribute ~2 in absolute-value arithmetic. The adjoint index acheck/abs(vbar) at the node is therefore ~4/u_j^2 and the cover separation ~2/u_j, both set by delta alone.\n\nx0 alternates delta, 1, delta, 1, ..., so every u_j is delta and every unit carries the same index. The units overlap — x_j feeds both u_{j-1} and u_j — so the Hessian is tridiagonal rather than block diagonal, and the separation is a property of each node rather than of the problem size: it is the same at every n, which is what makes the family usable in a size sweep.\n\nEach input reaches the output along one path per unit, so no intermediate gradient cancels: its absolute-value bound is exact, and the family isolates the cancellation in the adjoints. sigma spreads the log-magnitudes of the odd coordinates, spreading the per-unit index without touching the mechanism.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.adjoint_cancellation"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.cancellation-Tuple{Integer}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"cancellation(n; m=n, delta=1e-3, aligned=false, support=1:n, sigma=0.0, rng=MersenneTwister(1)) -> CoverProblem\n\nAn adversarial pair of programs built from m quartets of squared linear forms whose signed sum cancels exactly, riding on a delta-weighted rank-1 quadratic:\n\nacc = (delta/2) * (q'x)^2\nfor j in 1:m\n    acc += 0.5*(a_j'xS)^2 + 0.5*(b_j'xS)^2 + s*(c_j'xS)^2 + s*(d_j'xS)^2\nend\n\nwhere xS = x[support], c_j = (a_j+b_j)/sqrt(2), d_j = (a_j-b_j)/sqrt(2), and a_j, b_j are drawn orthogonal to x0[support]. At s = -0.5 (aligned = false, the default) the rotation identity c_j c_j' + d_j d_j' = a_j a_j' + b_j b_j' makes every quartet's contribution to the Hessian cancel exactly; at s = 0.5 (aligned = true) the same identity makes it add. The two variants differ only in the sign of the two constants multiplying the c_j and d_j squares: every node of the tape up to that sign carries the same value in both, because every quartet linear form vanishes at x0 (orthogonality to x0[support]), so f, its gradient, and the magnitude of every elemental partial and adjoint coincide between the two variants — only the curvature they carry differs.\n\nClosed forms (the Hessian is constant in x):\n\nH_cancel  = delta * q*q'\nH_aligned = delta * q*q' + sum_j 2*(a_j*a_j' + b_j*b_j')   (scattered onto `support`)\n\nThe minimal cover of H_cancel is hstar = sqrt(delta) .* abs.(q) = sqrt.(diag(H_cancel)) (a rank-1 |H|, since H_cancel is itself rank 1). The quadrature merge of the cancel variant attains sqrt.(diag(H_aligned)) exactly — the sign flip that cancels the Hessian does not touch the elementwise bound B, which sees only magnitudes — so its looseness against hstar is sqrt.(kappa) with kappa_i = diag(H_aligned)_i / diag(H_cancel)_i, the per-coordinate cancellation index.\n\nm sets the number of quartets and hence the size of kappa on the support (more quartets, more cancellation, larger kappa); delta sets the scale of the surviving rank-1 term relative to the quartets; support restricts the quartets to a coordinate subset, leaving off-support coordinates untouched by any cancellation; sigma widens the log-magnitude spread of the quartet coefficients and of q. Two calls that agree in n, m, delta, support, sigma, and rng state draw the same x0, q, and quartets regardless of aligned, so a cancel/aligned pair built from fresh, identically seeded rngs shares every random draw and differs only in the program's two constants.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.cancellation"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.converged_inner_solve-Tuple{}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"converged_inner_solve(; steps=2) -> CoverProblem\n\nA bilevel program: steps Newton iterations on the inner problem min_u (u - x1)^2/2 + x2 u^4/4, with the output that same inner objective at the final iterate.\n\nBy the envelope theorem the output is stationary in u at the exact inner minimizer, so the adjoint through the final iterate collapses as the solve converges while the unsigned sum over paths does not. Newton doubles the accuracy each step, so the adjoint index grows superexponentially in steps. At steps = 0 the inner variable keeps its initial value and nothing cancels, which is the control.\n\nUnlike adjoint_cancellation this engages the intermediate-gradient channel too — the realistic case, since the same structure appears in implicit layers, self-consistent field iterations, and any objective differentiated through a converged inner solve. Its knob is steps rather than a size, so it takes no n and is not one of families.\n\nPast four steps the inner residual is at roundoff and a further step measures the floor rather than the mechanism.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.converged_inner_solve"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.deep_composition-Tuple{Integer}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"deep_composition(n; depth=4, width=4, sigma=0.0, planted=nothing, rng=MersenneTwister(1)) -> CoverProblem\n\nA depth-depth composition of layers, each of which sums its width channels into a single trunk node and then feeds that node back into all width channels:\n\nz = tanh.(W*x + b)\nrepeat depth times:\n    t = sum(z)                       # one node, `width` consumers\n    z = tanh.(a .* t .+ c .* z)\nf = sum(z.^2) / 2\n\nThe trunk is the reconvergence: every channel of layer l+1 depends on every channel of layer l, so the paths from an input to the output multiply by width per layer. width and depth therefore tune reconvergence and program length independently of n.\n\nsigma widens the input weights W and biases b only. The recurrent weights a and c keep unit scale (1/sqrt(width) per fan-in), because a wide spread there drives tanh into saturation and flattens the Hessian to nothing. Heterogeneous units belong on the inputs, which is where sigma puts them.\n\nA real planted replaces the biases by b = -W*xstar with xstar = x0 .+ planted .* randn(n), so that the first layer, and with it every later layer, vanishes at xstar: f(xstar) = 0, the minimum of f. For width >= n and W of full rank, xstar is the only zero of the first layer. planted sets the distance of x0 from xstar in units of the inputs; W, a, c, and x0 are those of the unplanted problem drawn from the same rng state. The problem carries xstar as p.xstar.\n\nNo closed-form Hessian; differentiate p.f.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.deep_composition"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.exact_hessian","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"exact_hessian(p::CoverProblem, x=p.x0) -> H\n\nThe analytic Hessian of p at x. Errors when p has none; test with has_exact_hessian first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.exact_hessian"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.exact_jacobian","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"exact_jacobian(p::JacobianProblem, x=p.x0) -> J\n\nThe analytic Jacobian of p at x. Errors when p has none; test with has_exact_jacobian first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.exact_jacobian"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.families-Tuple{}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"families(; sigma=0.0, seed=1) -> Vector{Pair{String,Any}}\n\nThe standard sweep: named generators n -> CoverProblem, one per family, differing in how severely the paths through the program reconverge. Pass the same n to each to compare families at fixed problem size, or sweep n within one to measure how a cover's looseness grows.\n\nThe three quadratic forms are seeded alike, so at a given n they carry the same Hessian and differ only as programs.\n\nEvery family but the last cancels in the intermediate gradients; adjcancel cancels in the adjoints, and is the only one whose looseness is set by a knob rather than by n.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.families"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.has_exact_hessian-Tuple{CoverProblem}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"has_exact_hessian(p::CoverProblem)\n\nWhether p carries an analytic Hessian. When it does not, a reference Hessian must come from elsewhere (ForwardDiff.hessian(p.f, p.x0)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.has_exact_hessian"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.has_exact_jacobian-Tuple{JacobianProblem}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"has_exact_jacobian(p::JacobianProblem)\n\nWhether p carries an analytic Jacobian. When it does not, a reference Jacobian must come from elsewhere (ForwardDiff.jacobian(p.r, p.x0)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.has_exact_jacobian"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.has_twin-Tuple{CoverProblem}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"has_twin(p::CoverProblem)\n\nWhether p carries an independent reference implementation of its program.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.has_twin"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.has_xstar-Tuple{CoverProblem}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"has_xstar(p::CoverProblem)\n\nWhether p carries a known global minimizer p.xstar.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.has_xstar"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.jacobian_cancellation-Tuple{Integer}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"jacobian_cancellation(n; nout=4, m=n, delta=1e-3, aligned=false, support=1:n, sigma=0.0,\n                      rng=MersenneTwister(1)) -> JacobianProblem\n\nAn adversarial pair of vector-valued programs built from m quartets of linear forms whose signed sum cancels exactly, riding on a delta-weighted rank-1 Jacobian and fanned out to nout outputs:\n\nT = sum_i [ 0.5*(a_i'xS) + 0.5*(b_i'xS) + s*(c_i'xS) + s*(d_i'xS) ]\nr_k = delta * q[k] * (p'x) + q[k] * T\n\nwhere xS = x[support] and c_i .+ d_i == a_i .+ b_i entrywise (an unequal split c_i = t_i .* (a_i .+ b_i), t_i entrywise in (0, 1), so all four forms are distinct and none is a scalar multiple of another). At s = -0.5 (aligned = false, the default) each quartet's contribution to every Jacobian entry cancels exactly; at s = 0.5 (aligned = true) it adds. The two variants differ only in the sign of the constant multiplying the c_i and d_i forms: every quartet form vanishes at x0 (orthogonality, arranged by the draw) and within each support column all four forms share one sign, so node values and the magnitude of every elemental partial coincide between the two variants — the surrogate Ũ and every merge built from it are identical, and only the signed Jacobian differs.\n\nClosed forms (the Jacobian is constant in x; ghat = sum_i (a_i .+ b_i) scattered onto support, g = abs.(ghat)):\n\nJ_cancel  = delta .* q * p'\nJ_aligned = q * (delta .* p .+ ghat)'\nUtilde    = abs.(q) * (delta .* abs.(p) .+ g)'     (both variants)\n\nThe sign alignment makes Utilde == abs.(J_aligned) exactly, and both Utilde and the two Jacobians are rank 1, so the minimal cover of each is exact and analytic. The per-entry cancellation index is constant across outputs: kappa_j = 1 + g_j / (delta * abs(p_j)) on the support, 1 off it — more quartets or smaller delta, larger kappa; support restricts the cancellation to a column subset, making the kappa profile anisotropic.\n\nsigma widens the log-magnitude spread of the quartet coefficients and of the output scales q, whose spread sets the sum/max merge gap sum(abs, q)/maximum(abs, q) — the effective output multiplicity, nout at sigma = 0 and approaching 1 as one output dominates. Two calls that agree in everything but aligned share every random draw and differ only in the program's one constant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.jacobian_cancellation"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.logscales-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, Integer}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"logscales(rng, n; sigma) -> d\n\nPositive scale factors with log.(d) normal of standard deviation sigma. Pass to rescale to give a problem's inputs heterogeneous units.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.logscales"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.lotka_volterra-Tuple{Integer}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"lotka_volterra(m; delta=0.3, decades=2.0, ntraj=3, h=0.1, nsteps=50, every=5,\n               rng=MersenneTwister(1)) -> CoverProblem\n\nLeast-squares estimation of the parameters of a generalized Lotka–Volterra system of m species,\n\ndy_i/dt = y_i * (r_i + sum_j A[i,j] * y_j),\n\nfrom its own simulated trajectories. The inputs are the parameters x = vcat(r, vec(A)), so n = m + m^2. The program integrates ntraj trajectories by nsteps classical Runge–Kutta steps of size h, observes every every-th step, and sums the squared misfits to the data, each divided by the equilibrium abundance of its species:\n\nf(x) = sum over trajectories, observations o, species i of ((y_i(o; x) - data_i(o)) / ybar_i)^2 / 2\n\nThe data come from planted parameters xstar, built around a positive equilibrium ybar whose entries are spread log-uniformly over decades decades: A[i,j] = B[i,j] / ybar[j] with B the negative identity plus a random perturbation whose symmetric part is kept negative definite, and r = -A*ybar, so ybar is a stable equilibrium. The parameters of the abundant and the rare species therefore differ in units by decades decades. Initial conditions are ybar times log-normal factors of standard deviation 1/2. The data are simulated by f's own arithmetic, so f(xstar) = 0 exactly, the minimum of f; the problem carries xstar as p.xstar.\n\nThe start is x0 = xstar .+ delta .* abs.(xstar) .* randn(n): delta is the relative perturbation of each parameter. Far from xstar the dynamics can blow up within the horizon, and f then returns Inf or NaN.\n\nEvery step of the integrator reuses every species' state in every right-hand side, so paths from a parameter to the output reconverge at each of the 4 * nsteps stages.\n\nNo closed-form Hessian; differentiate p.f.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.lotka_volterra"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.mlp-Tuple{Any}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"mlp(sizes; batch=4, sigma=0.0, activation=tanh, rng=MersenneTwister(1)) -> CoverProblem\n\nThe squared-error loss of a feedforward network, with the parameters as inputs. For sizes = (n0, n1, ..., nK), layer l = 1:K has a weight matrix W_l of size n_l x n_{l-1} and a bias vector b_l of length n_l, flattened (W1, b1, W2, b2, ..., WK, bK) into one vector of length p = sum_l n_l*(n_{l-1}+1):\n\nz = data column                    # n0, fixed\nfor l = 1:K-1\n    z = activation.(W_l*z .+ b_l)  # hidden layers\ny = W_K*z .+ b_K                   # output layer, linear\nf = sum_over_columns( sum(abs2, y - target) ) / 2\n\nbatch input/target column pairs are drawn once at construction and closed over; only the parameters vary thereafter. f is written in array notation: a matvec whose TVar factor meets a constant factor (the data column feeding layer 1, or feeding layer K directly when K = 1) records one linear tape node per row, while a matvec between two TVar factors (every hidden-to-hidden transition) records one inner-product node per row. Each column's error sums as one linear node, and the batch total sums as another, so the tape stays short despite the array notation.\n\ntwin computes the same loss by an explicit scalar loop: every row's dot product is the left-to-right chain w[1]*z[1] + w[2]*z[2] + ... in the order a dot node's parents follow, bias and activation are applied per scalar, and every sum (over output units, over the batch) is an explicit binary + chain. It therefore matches f node for node under a shared accumulation order, rather than taking an independent route through array operations as the other families' twins do.\n\nsigma spreads the log-magnitude of the initial weights and biases, mirroring deep_composition's convention; the weights are additionally scaled by 1/sqrt(n_{l-1}) so a wider input layer does not by itself inflate the initial activations.\n\nNo closed-form Hessian; differentiate p.f.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.mlp"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.noutputs-Tuple{JacobianProblem}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"noutputs(p::JacobianProblem)\n\nNumber of outputs of p.r.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.noutputs"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.nvars-Tuple{CoverProblem}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"nvars(p::CoverProblem)\n\nNumber of inputs of p.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.nvars"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.quadratic-Tuple{Integer}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"quadratic(n; rank=n, form=:entrywise, sigma=0.0, rng=MersenneTwister(1)) -> CoverProblem\n\nThe quadratic f(x) = x'Ax/2 with A = W*W' positive semidefinite of the given rank, where W is n × rank and sigma sets the spread of its coefficients' log-magnitudes. The Hessian is A, independent of x.\n\nform selects how the same function is written as a program, which is what the cover sees:\n\n:entrywise — sum_{i,j} A[i,j] * x[i] * x[j] / 2. Every entry of A gets its own nonlinear node, and all n^2 of them reconverge on the inputs.\n:matvec — y = A*x scalarized, then x'y/2. The n products x[i]*y[i] reconverge through the rows of A.\n:factored — s = W'x, then sum(s.^2)/2. Only rank nonlinear nodes, each carrying a whole rank-1 layer of the Hessian, so no two nonlinear contributions overlap.\n\nFor rank = 1 the factored form's minimal cover is known exactly: H = w*w' forces h >= abs.(w), and abs.(w) = sqrt.(diag(A)) attains it.\n\nTwo calls that agree in n, rank, sigma, and rng state produce the same A and the same x0 in every form, so a comparison across forms isolates the effect of program structure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.quadratic"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.random_dag-Tuple{Integer}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"random_dag(n; nops=4n, sharing=0.5, sigma=0.0, rng=MersenneTwister(1)) -> CoverProblem\n\nA random program of n inputs and at most nops elementary operations, drawn from +, -, *, scaling, tanh, squaring, and sin. The output sums every node that no other node consumes, so every input reaches the output. At sharing = 0 generation also stops as soon as a single unconsumed node remains (once any curvature-carrying op has been drawn): the only way to grow a tree past that point is one long unary chain, whose accumulated derivative factors wander into under- or overflow. A draw whose ops all happen to be linear gets one final squaring node, so every generated program has a nonzero Hessian.\n\nsharing controls subexpression reuse, and with it the reconvergence the cover has to contend with. Each operand is either an unconsumed node (probability 1 - sharing) or an arbitrary existing node (probability sharing). At sharing = 0 no node is ever consumed twice, so the program's graph is a tree: each input reaches each node along at most one path. At sharing = 1 operands are drawn from the whole program and paths reconverge freely. Reaching the output is graph reachability, not influence: with reuse, an input can enter only linearly (a zero Hessian row), or even through linear paths whose unit weights cancel exactly (a zero gradient entry as well). Both are rare, correct, and must be tolerated by downstream code.\n\nA node whose magnitude at x0 leaves [1e-3, 1e3] is followed by a normalizing scale node, so * and ^2 chains can neither overflow nor collapse to a numerically zero derivative. A tanh input outside [-2, 2] is likewise normalized to magnitude 1 first: 1 - tanh(v)^2 underflows to exactly zero for large v, and a derivative-dead node erases its whole subtree from the gradient and the Hessian. These guards are what keep the program nonlinear at every length, and what make the recipe safe to replay at points other than x0.\n\nNo closed-form Hessian; differentiate p.f.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.random_dag"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/problems/#DerivativeCoverProblems.rescale-Tuple{CoverProblem, AbstractVector}","page":"DerivativeCoverProblems","text":"rescale(p::CoverProblem, d::AbstractVector) -> CoverProblem\n\nThe same function in rescaled input coordinates: g(y) = f(y ./ d) at y0 = d .* x0, whose Hessian is Diagonal(inv.(d)) * H * Diagonal(inv.(d)).\n\nThis is the input-units axis of dynamic range, and it is exactly the transformation a scale-invariant cover must be covariant under: a cover h of p at x0 must reappear as h ./ d for the rescaled problem. Take d from logscales for a random spread.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCoverProblems.rescale"},{"category":"section","location":"#DerivativeCovers","page":"Introduction","text":"DerivativeCovers computes rank-one bounds on the magnitudes of the entries of a program's Hessian or Jacobian, without forming either matrix. For a scalar function f whose Hessian at x0 is H, a Hessian cover is a nonnegative vector h satisfying\n\nH_ij le h_i h_j quad textfor all  i j\n\nFor a vector-valued function whose Jacobian at x0 is J, a Jacobian cover is a pair of nonnegative vectors a and b satisfying J_kj le a_k b_j for all k, j.\n\nThese are the covers of MatrixCovers, which computes them from an explicit matrix. Forming a Hessian takes O(nL) work for a program with n inputs and L operations. DerivativeCovers records the program once and computes a Hessian cover from the recording in O(L) work, a small multiple of the cost of the gradient, which it returns as well.\n\nThe bound is certified: it holds for the exact derivatives at x0, up to the floating-point rounding of the computation. When the program is recorded over intervals, the bound holds at every point of the box, with no rounding caveat (see Covers over a box).","title":"DerivativeCovers"},{"category":"section","location":"#Why-covers?","page":"Introduction","text":"Covers provide the natural scales of an optimization problem. The variables of a problem often have different units, and many quantities used by optimization algorithms depend on the choice of those units. Changing the units of the variables, x → D*x for a positive diagonal D, changes the Hessian to inv(D)*H*inv(D), and the cover changes with it, h → inv(D)*h. Quantities formed by dividing by the cover are therefore independent of the units.\n\nConsider fitting a decaying exponential to data. The two parameters, an amplitude and a time constant in seconds, differ in size by six orders of magnitude:\n\njulia> using DerivativeCovers, LinearAlgebra\n\njulia> ts = [0.0, 0.001, 0.002, 0.004, 0.008];    # sample times, in seconds\n\njulia> ys = 2000 .* exp.(-ts ./ 0.003);            # amplitude 2000, time constant 3 ms\n\njulia> loss(p) = sum((p[1] * exp(-ts[i] / p[2]) - ys[i])^2 for i in eachindex(ts)) / 2;\n\njulia> p0 = [1800.0, 0.0035];\n\njulia> c = cover_hessian(loss, p0);\n\njulia> round.(c.h_sum; sigdigits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 3.118\n 1.123e6\n\njulia> round.(c.grad; sigdigits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n -246.8\n    3.456e6\n\ncover_hessian returns the gradient c.grad along with the cover c.h_sum. The two entries of each have different units: the first entry of the gradient is in units of the loss per unit of amplitude, and the second in units of the loss per second. Expressing the time constant in milliseconds instead of seconds changes the second entry of both the gradient and the cover by a factor of 1000, while leaving the problem unchanged:\n\njulia> loss_ms(q) = loss([q[1], q[2] / 1000]);   # time constant in milliseconds\n\njulia> q0 = [1800.0, 3.5];\n\njulia> cms = cover_hessian(loss_ms, q0);\n\njulia> round.(cms.h_sum ./ c.h_sum; sigdigits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 1.0\n 0.001\n\njulia> round.(cms.grad ./ c.grad; sigdigits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 1.0\n 0.001\n\nThe cover changed exactly as the units did, so the gradient measured relative to the cover, g ./ h, did not change at all:\n\njulia> c.grad ./ c.h_sum ≈ cms.grad ./ cms.h_sum\ntrue\n\nEach entry of g ./ h has the units of the square root of the loss, whatever the units of its parameter, so its entries can be compared with one another and combined into a norm. The entries of g itself cannot: a sum such as sum(abs2, g) adds quantities with different units, and which entry dominates it depends on the choice of units.\n\nThe cover was computed without forming the Hessian. For this small problem we can form it from Hessian-vector products (hvp) and confirm the bound:\n\njulia> E = eachcol(Matrix(1.0I, 2, 2));\n\njulia> H = reduce(hcat, [hvp(loss, p0, e) for e in E]);\n\njulia> all(abs.(H) .<= c.h_sum .* c.h_sum')\ntrue\n\nThe same reasoning applies to the Hessian. Dividing it by the cover gives a matrix Hhat whose entries lie in [-1, 1] and do not depend on the units:\n\njulia> Hhat = H ./ (c.h_sum .* c.h_sum');\n\njulia> round.(Hhat; digits=3)\n2×2 Matrix{Float64}:\n 0.205  0.072\n 0.072  0.073\n\njulia> Hms = reduce(hcat, [hvp(loss_ms, q0, e) for e in E]);\n\njulia> Hms ./ (cms.h_sum .* cms.h_sum') ≈ Hhat\ntrue\n\nUsing a cover describes these and other uses.","title":"Why covers?"},{"category":"section","location":"#Which-function-to-use","page":"Introduction","text":"Function Bounds Cost Certified\ncover_hessian Hessian of a scalar function O(L) h_sum yes; h_var no\ncover_hessian(WithDiagnostics(), …) adds a tighter cover h_quad and an elementwise bound O(nL) time, O(n^2) memory yes\nDerivativeCovers.cover_hybrid Hessian, between h_sum and h_quad O(GL) for G groups yes\nforward_cover Hessian, without recording the program O(nL) time, O(n) memory per live variable yes\ncover_nls Hessian and Gauss–Newton matrix of tfrac12lVert r(x)rVert^2 O(L); O(GL) with G residual channels h, h_gn yes; h_var, h_var_gn no\ncover_jacobian Jacobian of a vector-valued function a few O(L) sweeps yes\nDerivativeCovers.cover_jacobian_hybrid Jacobian, tighter than the default O(GL) for G groups yes\n\nFor most uses, cover_hessian and cover_jacobian are the place to start. The O(L) cover h_sum can be loose when many paths through the program reconverge; Computing covers describes the options that trade cost for tightness, and Tightness describes how to measure it.","title":"Which function to use"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Introduction","text":"DerivativeCovers is not yet registered. Install it from its subdirectory in the repository:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/HolyLab/DerivativeCovers.jl\", subdir=\"DerivativeCovers\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Organization-of-this-manual","page":"Introduction","text":"Computing covers describes each kind of cover and its cost.\nUsing a cover shows what a cover bounds and how it enters numerical algorithms.\nScale covariance states when a cover transforms exactly with the units of the inputs.\nTightness describes how to measure a cover's looseness and where it comes from.\nCovers over a box extends the bounds from a point to a box of inputs.\nPrograms and new operations describes what a program may contain and how to add operations.\nThe reference pages list the public API: Computing and using covers, the Extension interface, and the companion package DerivativeCoverProblems. Names that are public but not exported, such as equilibrate, are called with the DerivativeCovers. prefix.","title":"Organization of this manual"},{"category":"section","location":"covers/#Computing-covers","page":"Computing covers","text":"","title":"Computing covers"},{"category":"section","location":"covers/#How-a-Hessian-cover-is-built","page":"Computing covers","text":"A program is a sequence of elementary operations, each computing a new value v_k = φ_k(v_{p_1}, v_{p_2}, …) from earlier values. Differentiating twice with the chain rule expresses the Hessian of the output as a sum of one term per operation,\n\nH = sum_k bar v_k sum_ij C^k_ij nabla v_p_i nabla v_p_j^mathsf T\n\nwhere C^k holds the second partial derivatives of φ_k with respect to its arguments, nabla v_p_i is the gradient of argument i with respect to the inputs (its intermediate gradient), and bar v_k is the derivative of the output with respect to v_k (the adjoint). Operations that are linear in their arguments, such as +, have C^k = 0 and contribute nothing.\n\nDerivativeCovers bounds each term by a rank-one matrix s_k s_k^mathsf T, built from a cover of the small matrix bar v_k C^k and from bounds on the magnitudes of the intermediate gradients. These local covers are then combined into a cover of H. Two ways of combining them are provided:\n\nthe sum merge h_sum = Σ_k s_k, which is valid because the cross terms of (sum_k s_k)(sum_k s_k)^mathsf T are nonnegative;\nthe quadrature merge h_quad[i] = sqrt(Σ_k s_k[i]^2), which is valid by the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality and never exceeds h_sum.\n\nThe sum merge is linear in the local covers, so it can be accumulated in a single reverse sweep over the recorded program, at a cost of O(L) for a program of L operations. The quadrature merge needs each s_k as an explicit vector of length n, at a cost of O(nL).","title":"How a Hessian cover is built"},{"category":"section","location":"covers/#Hessian-covers","page":"Computing covers","text":"cover_hessian takes a function and a point, or a recorded Tape and the index of its output (see Programs and new operations). By default it returns a HessianCover, computed in O(L):\n\njulia> using DerivativeCovers\n\njulia> f(x) = exp(x[1]) + x[1] * x[2]^2;\n\njulia> x0 = [0.5, 2.0];\n\njulia> c = cover_hessian(f, x0);\n\njulia> round.(c.h_sum; digits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 4.1125\n 2.4142\n\njulia> round.(c.grad; digits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 5.6487\n 2.0\n\nPassing WithDiagnostics as the first argument returns a HessianDiagnostics, which adds the quadrature merge h_quad and an explicit elementwise bound B ≥ abs.(H). These cost O(nL) time and O(n^2) memory, so they are not computed by default:\n\njulia> d = cover_hessian(WithDiagnostics(), f, x0);\n\njulia> round.(d.h_quad; digits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 3.1062\n 1.7321\n\njulia> round.(d.B; digits=4)\n2×2 Matrix{Float64}:\n 1.6487  4.0\n 4.0     1.0\n\nHere B equals abs.(H), because no cancellation occurs in this program.\n\nThe default result also includes the sign-averaged cover h_var, the root-sum-square of the same contributions that h_sum adds. It is the root-mean-square size of the sum if each contribution carried an independent random sign. h_var does not cover the Hessian: it can fall below the true scale where several contributions share a sign. It costs nothing extra, and the ratio h_sum ./ h_var indicates how much the contributions to each coordinate overlap.","title":"Hessian covers"},{"category":"section","location":"covers/#Between-the-two-merges","page":"Computing covers","text":"DerivativeCovers.cover_hybrid divides the local covers into G groups, sums within each group, and combines the group totals in quadrature. One group gives h_sum; one group per local term gives h_quad. The cost is O(GL), so G trades cost for tightness:\n\njulia> round.(DerivativeCovers.cover_hybrid(f, x0, 1); digits=4)    # the sum merge\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 4.1125\n 2.4142\n\njulia> round.(DerivativeCovers.cover_hybrid(f, x0, 3); digits=4)    # the quadrature merge\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 3.1062\n 1.7321\n\nnatoms reports the number of local terms on a tape, the group count at which the quadrature merge is reached.","title":"Between the two merges"},{"category":"section","location":"covers/#Least-squares-objectives","page":"Computing covers","text":"For a loss f(x) = tfrac12 sum_k r_k(x)^2, cover_nls takes the residual function r and returns an NLSCover with covers of both the full Hessian, h, and the Gauss–Newton matrix J^mathsf TJ, h_gn:\n\njulia> using DerivativeCovers\n\njulia> r(x) = [x[1] * x[2] - 1.0, exp(x[1]) - 2.0];\n\njulia> nc = cover_nls(r, [0.5, 2.0]);\n\njulia> round.(nc.h; digits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 3.7272\n 0.5\n\njulia> round.(nc.h_gn; digits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 3.6487\n 0.5\n\nBy default the residuals share one channel in the reverse sweep, for a cost of O(L). The ngroups keyword raises the number of channels to G, and the cost to O(GL); ngroups=nothing gives each of the m residuals its own channel, the tightest covers available. Summing residuals into one channel loosens h_gn[j] by at most sqrtn_j, where n_j is the number of residuals input j reaches. The sign-averaged covers h_var and h_var_gn are the analogs of h_var for cover_hessian.","title":"Least-squares objectives"},{"category":"section","location":"covers/#Jacobian-covers","page":"Computing covers","text":"cover_jacobian returns a JacobianCover with output weights a and input weights b_sum satisfying abs(J[k,j]) ≤ a[k] * b_sum[j]:\n\njulia> using DerivativeCovers\n\njulia> r(x) = [x[1] * x[2], exp(x[1]) - x[3], x[2] + 2x[3]];\n\njulia> jc = cover_jacobian(r, [0.5, 2.0, -1.0]);\n\njulia> J = [2.0 0.5 0; exp(0.5) 0 -1; 0 1 2];\n\njulia> all(abs.(J) .<= jc.a .* jc.b_sum')\ntrue\n\nA Jacobian cover is determined only up to a scalar factor (a → γ*a, b → b/γ), and beyond that the choice of a is free. The start keyword selects how a is chosen: :covariant (the default) makes the products a[k] * b[j] transform exactly under rescaling of the inputs and outputs, and :equal treats all outputs alike. The rounds keyword sets the number of refinement rounds. Alternatively, pass a fixed a.\n\nFor a given a, the tightest valid b is b_max[j] = maximum(abs(J[k,j]) / a[k]) (with abs(J) replaced by a bound computed from the program). The default computes the looser b_sum in O(L). cover_jacobian(WithDiagnostics(), …) also returns b_max and b_quad, which lies between b_max and b_sum, at a cost of O(mL). DerivativeCovers.cover_jacobian_hybrid interpolates between b_sum and b_max by grouping the outputs, at a cost of O(GL).","title":"Jacobian covers"},{"category":"section","location":"covers/#Propagating-a-cover-forward","page":"Computing covers","text":"forward_cover computes a Hessian cover without recording the program. Each variable carries its value, its exact intermediate gradient, and a cover of its own Hessian; the cover of the output is available when the program finishes. The memory required is O(n) for each variable that is live at a given moment, rather than for every operation of the program, so the method suits programs too long to record. The cost is O(nL) time.\n\nThe local covers need bounds on the magnitudes of the intermediate gradients and of the adjoints. The tape-based covers bound the intermediate gradients in absolute-value arithmetic, which loses any cancellation between the paths from an input to an operation; that is what allows the sum merge to be computed in a single O(L) sweep. A forward pass does the opposite: it carries the exact intermediate gradients, but it cannot know how the output depends on the current variable, so it bounds the adjoints in absolute-value arithmetic. Neither result is tighter in general:\n\njulia> using DerivativeCovers\n\njulia> g(x) = (d = sin(x[1]) - x[1]; u = d * x[2]; exp(u) - u);\n\njulia> x0 = [0.7, 1.3];\n\njulia> round.(cover_hessian(WithDiagnostics(), g, x0).h_quad; digits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 3.1673\n 0.0768\n\njulia> round.(forward_cover(g, x0).h_quad; digits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 2.0471\n 0.2928\n\nIn this program, d = sin(x[1]) - x[1] nearly cancels, so the absolute-value bound on the intermediate gradient of d overestimates the exact one, and the forward cover is tighter in x[1]; it is looser in x[2]. Tightness describes how to diagnose such losses.\n\nforward_cover stores its channels in arrays of the same type as x0. With a StaticVector from StaticArrays, no operation allocates; this is suited to programs with a handful of inputs, since the code is compiled for the specific number of inputs.","title":"Propagating a cover forward"},{"category":"section","location":"covers/#Reusing-a-recording","page":"Computing covers","text":"When several covers or products are needed at the same point, record the program once with tape_function and pass the tape to each function:\n\njulia> using DerivativeCovers\n\njulia> f(x) = exp(x[1]) + x[1] * x[2]^2;\n\njulia> t, out = tape_function(f, [0.5, 2.0]);\n\njulia> c = cover_hessian(t, out);\n\njulia> round.(hvp(t, out, [1.0, 0.0]); digits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 1.6487\n 4.0\n\nIf the adjoints of the tape have already been computed by other means, cover_hessian(CoverOnly(), t, vbar) computes the cover from them; see adjoints.","title":"Reusing a recording"},{"category":"section","location":"covers/#Checks-and-errors","page":"Computing covers","text":"All cover functions accept check=true (the default), which verifies each local cover against the second partial derivatives it bounds. A program that is not twice differentiable at the evaluation point raises an error that identifies the offending operation:\n\njulia> using DerivativeCovers\n\njulia> cover_hessian(x -> sqrt(x[1]) + x[2]^2, [0.0, 1.0])\nERROR: node 3 has non-finite second partials c=(Inf,); the program is not twice-differentiable at this point (node value 0.0, parent values (0.0,))\n[...]","title":"Checks and errors"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/covers/#Computing-and-using-covers","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"Names that are public but not exported are part of the supported API; call them with the module prefix, for example DerivativeCovers.equilibrate.\n\nPages = [\"covers.md\"]","title":"Computing and using covers"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/covers/#The-module","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"","title":"The module"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/covers/#Hessian-covers","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"","title":"Hessian covers"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/covers/#Least-squares-objectives","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"","title":"Least-squares objectives"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/covers/#Jacobian-covers","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"","title":"Jacobian covers"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/covers/#Result-types","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"","title":"Result types"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/covers/#Matrix-free-products","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"","title":"Matrix-free products"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/covers/#Using-covers","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"","title":"Using covers"},{"category":"module","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.DerivativeCovers","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"DerivativeCovers\n\nCompute certified rank-1 bounds on Hessians and Jacobians without forming either matrix.\n\nA Hessian cover is a nonnegative vector h satisfying abs(H[i,j]) <= h[i] * h[j] for every entry of H. cover_hessian records a scalar function on a Tape and computes such a cover, together with the gradient, in O(L) work for a tape of length L. The result is deterministic and certified.\n\nA cover is covariant under rescaling: if x -> D*x, then h -> inv(D)*h; if f -> c*f, then h -> sqrt(c)*h. That holds exactly on any program whose blocks all split on parent values carrying a scale of their own. Where one does not, the split falls back on declared input scales and stays covariant when those are rescaled alongside, scales -> D*scales; guards_any_block says whether a given program is in that case.\n\nusing DerivativeCovers imports the main entry points and result types. Public utilities such as equilibrate are not exported; call them with the DerivativeCovers. prefix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.DerivativeCovers"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.cover_hessian","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"cover_hessian([mode], t::Tape, outidx::Integer; check=true, scales=nothing, ratio=nothing)\ncover_hessian([mode], f, x0::AbstractVector; check=true, scales=nothing, ratio=nothing)\ncover_hessian(CoverOnly(), t::Tape, vbar::AbstractVector; check=true, scales=nothing, ratio=nothing)\n\nCompute a certified rank-1 cover of a scalar function's Hessian.\n\nPass either a tape and scalar output node, or a function and evaluation point. The default CoverOnly mode returns a HessianCover containing the gradient, the sum-merge cover, and the sign-averaged cover. WithDiagnostics additionally computes an explicit elementwise bound and a tighter quadrature cover.\n\nThe scales keyword declares the input scales the split falls back on where a block's parent values carry none, and ratio the threshold at which it does so; see split_block and split_ratio. Neither affects validity, only which of the valid covers is chosen, and guards_any_block says whether a program consults them at all. check=true verifies the local covering conditions.\n\nTwo merges are available:\n\nsum merge, h_sum = sum_k s_k: the cross terms of (sum_k s_k)(sum_k s_k)' are nonnegative slack;\nquadrature merge, h_quad[i] = sqrt(sum_atoms s_atom[i]^2): valid by Cauchy–Schwarz, and the minimal cover of the no-cancellation surrogate M = sum_atoms s_atom s_atom'. An atom (see atom_slots) is a whole node for unary and binary elementals and one parent pair for an inner-product node, so an inner-product node contributes q separate terms to the quadrature merge rather than one.\n\nBoth satisfy abs.(H) <= h * h'.\n\nThe gradient is in the tape's element type T and the covers in magtype(T). T may be a type whose values stand for sets, such as Interval{Float64} from IntervalArithmetic.jl: recording f over a box x0 = interval.(lo, hi) then gives covers (here Vector{Float64}) that hold at every point of the box, and a gradient whose entries enclose the gradient over the box. The sweeps over such a tape round every sum, product and square root upward (see boundtype), so the covers hold exactly; on a tape of points they hold up to the rounding of the sweeps. Control flow in f that the box does not decide, such as a comparison between overlapping intervals, raises an error, as does an elemental evaluated outside its domain somewhere on the box (see check_values).\n\nCoverOnly()\n\nThis mode takes one forward pass and one reverse sweep, or O(L) time and memory. The reverse sweep carries the ordinary adjoint, the absolute cover channel, and the squared channel together:\n\nvbar[p_i] += d_i * vbar[k]                              # signed channel: reverse-mode AD\nabar[p_i] += magnitude(d_i) * abar[k] + rho_k[i]        # absolute channel: the cover\nqbar[p_i] += magnitude(d_i)^2 * qbar[k] + rho_k[i]^2    # squared channel: h_var^2\n\nwith rho_k from local_cover_slots. Every channel of node k is final when the sweep reaches it: adjoint flow and theta seeds come only from k's consumers, which follow it on the tape and are processed first. That makes rho_k — which needs the finished vbar[k] — computable in place, and abar[1:ninputs] equal to abs_reverse(t, theta)[1:ninputs] up to roundoff.\n\nabar sums, over every path from a parent slot of a node to an input, the slot's seed times the product of the absolute first partials along the path. qbar sums the squares of the same path contributions, and h_var = sqrt.(qbar[1:ninputs]) is the sign-averaged cover of HessianCover, which does not cover the Hessian.\n\nA program that reaches the declared-scale fallback requires one additional forward sweep.\n\nPassing the node adjoints vbar =adjoints(t, outidx) in place of outidx runs the same sweep without the signed channel, for a caller that already holds the adjoints of a reverse pass. It returns the same HessianCover bit for bit, with grad = vbar[1:ninputs].\n\nWithDiagnostics()\n\nThis mode returns a HessianDiagnostics and costs O(n * L) time and O(n^2) memory. With check=true, it also verifies that the diagnostic and fused computations agree.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> f(x) = exp(x[1]) + x[1] * x[2]^2;\n\njulia> c = cover_hessian(f, [0.5, 2.0]);\n\njulia> round.(c.h_sum; digits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 4.1125\n 2.4142\n\njulia> round.(c.grad; digits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 5.6487\n 2.0\n\njulia> H = [exp(0.5) 4.0; 4.0 1.0];   # the Hessian of f at [0.5, 2.0]\n\njulia> all(abs.(H) .<= c.h_sum * c.h_sum')\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.cover_hessian"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.cover_hybrid","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"cover_hybrid(t::Tape, outidx::Integer, ngroups::Integer; check::Bool=true, scales=nothing, ratio=nothing) -> h\ncover_hybrid(t::Tape, outidx::Integer, groups::AbstractVector{<:Integer}; check::Bool=true, scales=nothing, ratio=nothing) -> h\ncover_hybrid(f, x0::AbstractVector, spec; check::Bool=true, scales=nothing, ratio=nothing) -> h\n\nCompute a Hessian cover by grouping cover contributions: contributions are summed within each group, then the group totals are combined in quadrature.\n\nAn integer ngroups groups the tape's cover atoms (see atom_slots), dealing them round-robin. ngroups=1 is the sum merge, and any ngroups at or above natoms(t) gives every atom a group of its own, which is exactly the quadrature merge h_quad of diagnostic cover_hessian. Because atoms rather than nodes are dealt, the whole range between the two merges stays reachable however the program was written: an elemental that carries several atoms — an inner product, a matrix-vector product — spreads them across groups like the scalar chain it stands for.\n\nAlternatively, groups[k] assigns tape node k to a group, holding all of that node's atoms together; cyclic_partition and level_partition build such partitions.\n\nEvery grouping produces a valid cover, and one group is always the sum merge. The computation costs O(G * L) time and memory for G groups. scales and ratio govern the split, as in cover_hessian, and check=true verifies local covers.\n\nExamples\n\nThe two endpoints, on a tape carrying three atoms:\n\njulia> f(x) = exp(x[1]) + x[1] * x[2]^2;\n\njulia> round.(DerivativeCovers.cover_hybrid(f, [0.5, 2.0], 1); digits=4)   # one group: the sum merge\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 4.1125\n 2.4142\n\njulia> round.(DerivativeCovers.cover_hybrid(f, [0.5, 2.0], 3); digits=4)   # a group per atom: the quadrature merge\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 3.1062\n 1.7321\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.cover_hybrid"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.forward_cover","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"forward_cover(f, x0::AbstractVector; scales=nothing, ratio=nothing, check=true) -> ForwardCover\nforward_cover(x::FVar) -> ForwardCover\n\nCompute a certified rank-1 cover of a scalar function's Hessian by propagating it forward, in one pass and with no tape.\n\nf must accept a vector of FVars and return one, using operations FVar supports. Per variable the propagation carries the value, the intermediate gradient ∇v, a squared cover of ∇²v, and the scalar declared scale sigma; the recursions are\n\n∇v_k    = sum_i d_i * ∇v_i\nsigma_k = sum_i magnitude(d_i) * sigma_i\nG_k     = sum_i magnitude(d_i) * G_i + sum over atoms of (sum_{i in atom} rho_i * magnitude.(∇v_i))^2\n\nwith rho the same local cover a reverse sweep builds, except that the adjoint factor is 1: a forward pass cannot know how the output depends on the node it is at. The cover is sqrt.(G).\n\nThe gradient is in the element type T = float(eltype(x0)) and the cover in magtype(T). As for cover_hessian, x0 may be a box of intervals: the gradient then encloses the gradient over the box and the cover holds at every point of it, the propagation rounding its bounds upward (see boundtype). An elemental evaluated outside its domain somewhere on the box is an error (see check_values).\n\nThe second form returns the cover of any variable reached along the way, the propagation having maintained the invariant at every one of them, not only at the output.\n\nComputational cost and tightness\n\nO(n*L) time for n inputs and L operations, and O(n) memory per live variable — no tape, and nothing proportional to the length of the program. Its main advantage is therefore the ability to process programs that are too long to record.\n\nThe channels are stored in whatever x0 is made of, so a StaticVector point propagates in static arrays and no node allocates at all. That is worth doing for a handful of inputs and not for many: the propagation is then compiled for the exact input count, which for large n costs more in compilation than it saves at run time. A Vector point propagates in Vectors and compiles once for every size.\n\nThe cover keeps the signs of the intermediate gradients ∇v, which a forward pass carries exactly, and discards the signs of the adjoints, which it cannot see: each atom is weighted by the unsigned sum over paths from its node to the output rather than by the node's adjoint. That makes it incomparable with the quadrature merge h_quad of HessianDiagnostics, which discards the signs of the intermediate gradients and keeps those of the adjoints; which is tighter is a property of the program.\n\nscales and ratio govern the split exactly as in cover_hessian: the propagation carries both of the references split_block reads, so it splits by the same rule a tape does and its cover is comparable with the tape's coordinate by coordinate. See forward_scale. check=true verifies every local cover.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> f(x) = exp(x[1]) + x[1] * x[2]^2;\n\njulia> c = forward_cover(f, [0.5, 2.0]);\n\njulia> round.(c.grad; digits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 5.6487\n 2.0\n\njulia> H = [exp(0.5) 4.0; 4.0 1.0];   # the Hessian of f at [0.5, 2.0]\n\njulia> all(abs.(H) .<= c.h_quad * c.h_quad')\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.forward_cover"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.CoverOnly","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"CoverOnly()\n\nReturn only the covers a sweep-tier computation yields: the gradient, sum-merge cover, and sign-averaged cover from cover_hessian at O(L), or the gradient, two covers, and two scales from cover_nls at O(G * L) for G residual channels, O(L) by default. This is the default mode of both.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.CoverOnly"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.WithDiagnostics","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"WithDiagnostics()\n\nReturn the extended result, adding the quantities that must be materialized per input or per output: a HessianDiagnostics from cover_hessian, with the elementwise bound, quadrature merge, and an independent consistency check, at O(n * L) time and O(n^2) memory; an NLSDiagnostics from cover_nls, with the residual-sensitivity surrogate, at O(m * L) time and O(m * n) memory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.WithDiagnostics"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.cover_nls","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"cover_nls([mode], r, x0; ngroups=1, check=true, scales=nothing, ratio=nothing)\n\nCompute covers for the least-squares loss f(x) = ½∑ₖ rₖ(x)² at x0.\n\nr must map a vector of TVars to a vector of residuals. The default CoverOnly mode returns an NLSCover holding a cover h of the full loss Hessian, a cover h_gn of the Gauss–Newton matrix JᵀJ, and the sign-averaged covers h_var <= h of the full Hessian and h_var_gn <= h_gn of JᵀJ (the latter from gramvariance), which cover the mean magnitudes of a sign ensemble rather than these matrices. WithDiagnostics returns an NLSDiagnostics, which adds the absolute-sensitivity surrogate Utilde for a further O(m * L).\n\nngroups sets the number of residual channels G, and with it the entire cost of the CoverOnly mode: O(G * L) time and O(G * n) memory. The residuals are spread cyclically over the G channels, and their sensitivities are summed within a channel before the quadrature merge. The default 1 sums them all, so h_gn is the gramcover of the residuals and both covers cost O(L). ngroups=nothing gives every residual a channel of its own (G = m), the tightest pair of covers available, at O(m * L); its h_gn[j] is smaller than the default's by a factor of at most sqrt(n_j), where n_j is the number of residuals input j reaches. That factor varies with j, so it changes the profile of the cover as well as its level. The two sign-averaged covers take one further O(L) sweep and do not depend on G.\n\nscales and ratio govern the split, as in cover_hessian, and reach only the residual-curvature channel, and through it h and h_var: the residual channels carry unit atoms, which admit no split, so none of h_gn, h_var_gn and Utilde depends on them. check=true verifies the local covering conditions, and under WithDiagnostics also verifies h_gn against Utilde.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> r(x) = [x[1] * x[2] - 1.0, exp(x[1]) - 2.0];\n\njulia> nc = cover_nls(r, [0.5, 2.0]);\n\njulia> round.(nc.h; digits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 3.7272\n 0.5\n\njulia> round.(nc.h_gn; digits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 3.6487\n 0.5\n\njulia> all(nc.h_gn .<= nc.h)\ntrue\n\njulia> round.(nc.h_var; digits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 2.7014\n 0.5\n\njulia> all(nc.h_var_gn .<= nc.h_var .<= nc.h)\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.cover_nls"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.gramvariance","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"gramvariance(t::Tape, outidxs) -> h\ngramvariance(t::Tape, outidxs, w::AbstractVector) -> h\ngramvariance(t::Tape, outidxs, W::Diagonal) -> h\n\nSign-averaged cover, the root-mean-square column scale, of the Gram matrix G = J'*W*J of the Jacobian J of the tape outputs outidxs: a nonnegative vector h, one entry per input (1:t.ninputs), with\n\nh[j] = sqrt(∑_k abs(w[k]) * Ũ⁽²⁾[k,j]),\n\nwhere Ũ⁽²⁾[k,j] sums, over the paths from input j to output k, the squared product of the local partial magnitudes along the path. W defaults to the identity, and a vector w means W = Diagonal(w), indexed like outidxs; only abs.(w) enters, so an output with zero weight contributes nothing, and a repeated entry of outidxs adds its weights.\n\nModel each parent slot's local partial as carrying an independent, equiprobable sign (the sign ensemble). Distinct paths then contribute uncorrelated terms, so E[J[k,j]^2] = Ũ⁽²⁾[k,j] and h[j]^2 = E[(J'*abs.(W)*J)[j,j]]: h is the root-mean-square column norm of sqrt.(abs.(W))*J, the column (MINPACK) scaling of the Gram matrix in expectation. The entries of J for distinct outputs are uncorrelated too, so off-diagonal weights contribute nothing to this expectation; a general W::AbstractMatrix is an error. On an interval tape the magnitudes are the largest over the box, so h bounds the pointwise h at every point of the box.\n\nh covers the ensemble's mean magnitudes, E[abs(G[i,j])] <= h[i]*h[j], not G itself: where several paths reach one entry of J with the same sign it can fall below that column's norm. Per entry Ũ⁽²⁾ <= Ũ.^2 (a sum of squares is at most the square of the sum), where Ũ is the absolute-sensitivity surrogate (jacobian_surrogate), so\n\nh[j] <= h_gn[j] = sqrt(∑_k abs(w[k]) * Ũ[k,j]^2) <= gramcover(t, outidxs, w)[j].\n\nUnweighted, h_gn is the Gauss–Newton cover of cover_nls with ngroups=nothing. Where each entry of J has a single path, Ũ⁽²⁾ = Ũ.^2 = abs2.(J) on a point tape, and h equals h_gn and the column norms of sqrt.(abs.(W))*J. Rescaling the inputs, x -> D*x, gives h -> inv(D)*h; rescaling the outputs by a diagonal C together with w -> w ./ C.^2 leaves h unchanged.\n\nh comes from one reverse sweep with squared local partials (the squared channel of dependency_sweep, without its linear channel) seeded abs(w[k]) at output node outidxs[k], so it costs O(L) for a tape of length L, where h_gn needs one channel per output. gramvariance! writes h into a supplied vector.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> r(x) = [x[1] * x[2], exp(x[1]) - x[3], x[2] + 2x[3]];\n\njulia> t, outidxs = tape_function(r, [0.5, 2.0, -1.0]);\n\njulia> round.(DerivativeCovers.gramvariance(t, outidxs); digits=4)   # column norms of J\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 2.592\n 1.118\n 2.2361\n\njulia> round.(DerivativeCovers.gramvariance(t, outidxs, [1.0, 0.0, 4.0]); digits=4)\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 2.0\n 2.0616\n 4.0\n\njulia> t2, o2 = tape_function(x -> [x[1] * x[1]], [3.0]);   # two paths, J = [6.0;;]\n\njulia> round.(DerivativeCovers.gramvariance(t2, o2); digits=4)   # sqrt(3^2 + 3^2)\n1-element Vector{Float64}:\n 4.2426\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.gramvariance"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.gramvariance!","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"gramvariance!(h, t::Tape, outidxs) -> h\ngramvariance!(h, t::Tape, outidxs, w::AbstractVector) -> h\ngramvariance!(h, t::Tape, outidxs, W::Diagonal) -> h\n\nWrite gramvariance's scale of the Gram matrix of the tape outputs outidxs into h and return it. h must be indexed like the inputs (eachindex(h) == 1:t.ninputs).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.gramvariance!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.nls_loss_tape","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"nls_loss_tape(r, x0) -> (t, outidx, residx, sqidx)\n\nRecord the least-squares loss f = ½∑ₖ rₖ(x)² at x0, where r maps a vector of TVars to a vector of residual TVars. Returns the Tape, the loss output node index, the node indices residx of the residuals rₖ, and the node indices sqidx of their squares rₖ².\n\nr may return residuals that are input or duplicated nodes; each is squared into its own fresh node, so sqidx has one distinct entry per residual regardless. A constant residual — a fixed offset of the loss, contributing ½c² to f and a zero row to J — gets a node of its own (constantnode); an all-constant residual vector depends on no parameter and is an error, as is a recording that check_values rejects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.nls_loss_tape"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.gn_ab","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"gn_ab(nls::NLSDiagnostics) -> (a, b)\n\nA valid rank-1 Jacobian cover (a, b) — a_k b_j ≥ |J_{kj}| — read off the residual sensitivities of nls: a = ones(m) and b[j] = maximum(Utilde[:, j]), so a_k b_j = b_j ≥ ũ_{rₖ,j} ≥ |J_{kj}|. b is the max merge for unit output weights: the tightest cover of J whose row scale the unweighted least-squares loss fixes at one. It serves to cross-check a general asymmetric Jacobian cover; the symmetric Gauss–Newton cover of JᵀJ is nls.h_gn.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.gn_ab"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.cover_jacobian","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"cover_jacobian([mode,] t::Tape, outidxs; start=:covariant, rounds=4, a=nothing, check=true)\ncover_jacobian([mode,] r, x0::AbstractVector; ...)\n\nAsymmetric rank-1 cover of a vector-valued function's Jacobian: weights with a[k] * b[j] >= abs(J[k,j]). Pass either a tape and its output nodes, or a function and an evaluation point.\n\na is chosen by the balancing construction: start is :covariant, whose products on the support transform exactly under a rescaling of the program's inputs, outputs and intermediates, or :equal, which is permutation equivariant instead; rounds alternation rounds then refine it, and rounds = nothing runs balance_cover's alternation to convergence instead. Passing a yourself fixes the weights and rules out start and rounds. a must be strictly positive on every output some input reaches and may be zero on the others, which then contribute to no merge.\n\nModes\n\nCoverOnly() (the default) returns a JacobianCover from O(L) sweeps, never forming the surrogate. Checking a pair against the surrogate is what the other mode is for, so check has no effect here; a factor that vanishes on a row or column the program actually uses is an error either way.\nWithDiagnostics() returns a JacobianDiagnostics: the same a and b_sum, plus the surrogate and the two tighter merges, at O(m * L). check then verifies b_max against the surrogate.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> r(x) = [x[1] * x[2], exp(x[1]) - x[3], x[2] + 2x[3]];\n\njulia> jc = cover_jacobian(WithDiagnostics(), r, [0.5, 2.0, -1.0]; a = ones(3));\n\njulia> round.(jc.b_max; digits=4)\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 2.0\n 1.0\n 2.0\n\njulia> all(jc.b_max .<= jc.b_quad .<= jc.b_sum)\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.cover_jacobian"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.cover_jacobian_hybrid","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"cover_jacobian_hybrid(t::Tape, outidxs, groups::AbstractVector{<:Integer}; kwargs...) -> (; a, b)\ncover_jacobian_hybrid(t::Tape, outidxs, ngroups::Integer; kwargs...) -> (; a, b)\ncover_jacobian_hybrid(r, x0::AbstractVector, groups | ngroups; kwargs...) -> (; a, b)\n\nCompute a Jacobian cover by grouping outputs.\n\ngroups[k] assigns output k to a group. Absolute sensitivities are summed within each group, then the group totals are combined with max. Alternatively, pass ngroups to assign outputs cyclically.\n\nEvery grouping is valid. One group reproduces b_sum; one group per output reproduces b_max. The cost is O(G * L) for G = maximum(groups), without forming the full surrogate.\n\nThe output weights are cover_jacobian's, chosen by the same keywords:\n\nstart: :covariant (the default) or :equal.\nrounds: alternation rounds refining them, 4 by default.\na: fixed weights of your own instead, strictly positive on every output some input reaches and optionally zero on the others. It rules out start and rounds.\ncheck = true: verify the returned b against the surrogate, at O(m * L).\n\nReturns the weights used and the grouped merge for them: a[k] * b[j] >= abs(J[k,j]).\n\nExamples\n\nThe two endpoints, on a program with three outputs:\n\njulia> r(x) = [x[1] * x[2], exp(x[1]) - x[3], x[2] + 2x[3]];\n\njulia> t, outidxs = tape_function(r, [0.5, 2.0, -1.0]);\n\njulia> round.(DerivativeCovers.cover_jacobian_hybrid(t, outidxs, 1; a = ones(3)).b; digits=4)   # one group: b_sum\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 3.6487\n 1.5\n 3.0\n\njulia> round.(DerivativeCovers.cover_jacobian_hybrid(t, outidxs, 3; a = ones(3)).b; digits=4)   # per output: b_max\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 2.0\n 1.0\n 2.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.cover_jacobian_hybrid"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.HessianCover","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"HessianCover{T, M}\n\nThe standard result returned by cover_hessian. T is the element type of the Tape it was computed from, and M = magtype(T) the type of the cover. HessianCover{T} stands for HessianCover{T, magtype(T)}.\n\ngrad::Vector{T}: gradient of the scalar function at the evaluation point.\nh_sum::Vector{M}: certified Hessian cover computed with the sum merge.\nh_var::Vector{M}: the sign-averaged cover, the root-sum-square of the path contributions that h_sum adds. It covers the mean magnitude of the sign ensemble described below, not the Hessian.\n\nh_sum[i] adds one contribution per pair of a seeded parent slot and a path from that slot to input i: the slot's seed times the product of the absolute first partials along the path. h_var[i]^2 sums the squares of the same contributions. That is the expected square of their sum when each parent slot carries an independent, equiprobable sign, on its first partial and on its seed: the sign ensemble, the model gramvariance uses. The estimates satisfy h_var <= h_quad <= h_sum coordinatewise, with h_quad the quadrature merge of HessianDiagnostics. A coordinate reached by a single contribution has h_sum == h_var; one built by summing many overlapping contributions has h_sum grow like their sum and h_var like their root-sum-square, so the ratio h_sum ./ h_var grows with the effective overlap.\n\nA variable that enters one elemental through two slots is two paths whose contributions combine in quadrature, although in the program they share a sign: x[1] * x[1] seeds each of its two slots with 1, so h_sum = 2 adds them and h_var = √2, while x[1]^2, a unary elemental with a single slot, gives √2 for both. Where such paths reconverge further along, h_var falls below the exact scale: for x[1] * x[1] * x[1] at x = 3, h_var = √15 against sqrt(abs(f''(3))) = √18.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> cover_hessian(x -> x[1] * x[1], [3.0]).h_sum   # two slots, two paths\n1-element Vector{Float64}:\n 2.0\n\njulia> cover_hessian(x -> x[1] * x[1], [3.0]).h_var ≈ [sqrt(2)]\ntrue\n\njulia> cover_hessian(x -> x[1] * x[1] * x[1], [3.0]).h_var ≈ [sqrt(15)]   # |f''| = 18\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.HessianCover"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.HessianDiagnostics","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"HessianDiagnostics{T, M}\n\nThe extended result returned by cover_hessian(WithDiagnostics(), ...). T is the element type of the Tape it was computed from, which grad holds, and M = magtype(T) the type of the bound and the covers. HessianDiagnostics{T} stands for HessianDiagnostics{T, magtype(T)}.\n\ngrad: gradient of the scalar function.\nB: explicit elementwise bound satisfying B >= abs.(H).\nh_sum: cover computed with the sum merge.\nh_quad: tighter cover computed by combining cover atoms in quadrature.\nh_sum_rev: the absolute-value reverse sweep's own route to the sum merge, a consistency check.\n\nForming these diagnostics costs O(n * L) time and O(n^2) memory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.HessianDiagnostics"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.ForwardCover","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"ForwardCover{T, M, VG, VH}\n\nThe result of forward_cover, with T as the propagation's element type, M = magtype(T) the type of the cover, and VG, VH the channel storage the propagation ran in (see ForwardContext). ForwardCover{T} stands for ForwardCover{T, magtype(T)}.\n\ngrad::VG: the exact gradient of the output, in T.\nh_quad::VH: a certified Hessian cover formed by merging the cover atoms in quadrature, in M.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.ForwardCover"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.NLSCover","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"NLSCover{T, M}\n\nResult of cover_nls for the least-squares loss f = ½∑ₖ rₖ². T is the element type of the Tape it was computed from, and M = magtype(T) the type of the covers. NLSCover{T} stands for NLSCover{T, magtype(T)}.\n\ngrad::Vector{T}: gradient of the loss.\nh::Vector{M}: certified cover of the full Hessian JᵀJ + ∑ₖ rₖ ∇²rₖ.\nh_gn::Vector{M}: certified cover of the Gauss–Newton matrix JᵀJ; always h_gn <= h.\nh_var::Vector{M}: sign-averaged cover of the full Hessian, its root-mean-square column scale under random local signs, h_var² = h_var_gn² + c_var², where c_var is the sign-averaged form of the residual-curvature channel (one independent sign per parent slot, as for h_var of cover_hessian). It covers the sign ensemble's mean magnitude, not the Hessian; always h_var_gn <= h_var <= h.\nh_var_gn::Vector{M}: sign-averaged cover of JᵀJ, its root-mean-square column scale under random local signs, gramvariance of the residuals. It covers the sign ensemble's mean magnitude, not JᵀJ; always h_var_gn <= h_gn.\nm: the number of residuals.\n\nBoth covers are read off one grouped reverse sweep with G residual channels and one residual-curvature channel, so together they cost O(G * L) time and memory. With G = 1, the default, h_gn is the sum ∑ₖ ũ. With G = m, one residual per channel, h_gn is the per-residual quadrature √(∑ₖ ũ²), which equals sqrt.(diag(J'J)) when no signed paths cancel. The two scales share one further two-channel O(L) sweep through squared partials, whatever G is, so neither depends on G. h_var_gn equals h_gn (for G = m) where each entry of J has a single path, and h_var equals h_var_gn where the residuals have no curvature. h_var is the h_var of cover_hessian applied to the loss tape of nls_loss_tape.\n\nRescaling the inputs, x -> D*x with D diagonal, maps every channel h -> inv(D)*h on a program without guarded elementals; scales and ratio reach h and h_var only through the residual-curvature channel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.NLSCover"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.NLSDiagnostics","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"NLSDiagnostics{T, M}\n\nThe extended result returned by cover_nls(WithDiagnostics(), ...). T is the element type of the Tape it was computed from, which grad holds, and M = magtype(T) the type of the covers and of Utilde. NLSDiagnostics{T} stands for NLSDiagnostics{T, magtype(T)}. It carries the fields of NLSCover together with\n\nUtilde::Matrix{M}: m × n matrix of absolute residual sensitivities, satisfying Utilde[k,j] >= abs(J[k,j]).\n\nForming Utilde takes an m-channel reverse sweep of its own, O(m * L) time and O(m * n) memory, whatever number of residual channels the covers use.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.NLSDiagnostics"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.JacobianCover","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"JacobianCover{T}\n\nResult of cover_jacobian: a[k] * b_sum[j] >= abs(J[k,j]). T is magtype of the element type of the Tape it was computed from, which for a real floating-point tape is that element type itself.\n\na: output weights.\nb_sum: input weights from the sum merge, computed in one O(L) reverse sweep.\nroots, nties: the root data of the tape-potential start (see tape_potentials), or nothing when another start or an explicit a was used.\nm, n: numbers of outputs and inputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.JacobianCover"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.JacobianDiagnostics","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"JacobianDiagnostics{T}\n\nThe extended result returned by cover_jacobian(WithDiagnostics(), ...). T is magtype of the element type of the Tape it was computed from. It carries the fields of JacobianCover together with\n\nb_quad: the quadrature merge b_j = √(∑_k (Ũ_kj/a_k)²).\nb_max: the smallest valid b for this a, b_j = max_k Ũ_kj/a_k.\nUtilde: m × n absolute-sensitivity surrogate satisfying Utilde >= abs.(J).\n\nThe merges satisfy b_max <= b_quad <= b_sum coordinatewise. Forming Utilde takes an m-channel reverse sweep, O(m * L) time and O(m * n) memory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.JacobianDiagnostics"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.hvp","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"hvp(t::Tape, outidx::Integer, v::AbstractVector) -> Hv\nhvp(f, x0::AbstractVector, v::AbstractVector) -> Hv\nhvp!(Hv, t::Tape, outidx::Integer, v::AbstractVector) -> Hv\n\nHessian-vector product H*v of the taped program, formed without the Hessian by differentiating the adjoint recursion along v: one forward tangent sweep dv_k = sum_i (∂φ_k/∂v_i) dv_i seeded with v, then one reverse sweep carrying both the adjoints vbar and their tangents,\n\nvbardot_i += (∂φ_k/∂v_i) * vbardot_k + vbar_k * sum_j (∂²φ_k/∂v_i∂v_j) * dv_j.\n\nThe curvature term of each node comes from curvature_apply!, which every Curvature supplies in the form its structure allows — a PairedCurvature node reads its 2q nonzeros off the pairs rather than scanning a 2q × 2q matrix, without which a program built from q-long inner products would cost O(q) gradients instead of two. A node whose second partials are only declared (has_exact_partials is false) makes an exact product impossible: hvp throws on any tape containing one, unconditionally, rather than letting success depend on whether the node's adjoint happens to vanish. The tape method of scaled_spectral_radius inherits this restriction; its dense method does not.\n\nCosts about two gradients and O(L) memory.\n\nhvp! writes into Hv, which shares the axes of v. It saves the result allocation only: the two sweeps still allocate their own O(L) working vectors, so it is not allocation-free.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> f(x) = exp(x[1]) + x[1] * x[2]^2;\n\njulia> x0, v = [0.5, 2.0], [1.0, 0.0];\n\njulia> round.(hvp(f, x0, v); digits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 1.6487\n 4.0\n\njulia> h = cover_hessian(f, x0).h_sum;   # the cover bounds the product\n\njulia> all(abs.(hvp(f, x0, v)) .<= h .* (h' * abs.(v)))\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.hvp"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.hvp!","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"hvp(t::Tape, outidx::Integer, v::AbstractVector) -> Hv\nhvp(f, x0::AbstractVector, v::AbstractVector) -> Hv\nhvp!(Hv, t::Tape, outidx::Integer, v::AbstractVector) -> Hv\n\nHessian-vector product H*v of the taped program, formed without the Hessian by differentiating the adjoint recursion along v: one forward tangent sweep dv_k = sum_i (∂φ_k/∂v_i) dv_i seeded with v, then one reverse sweep carrying both the adjoints vbar and their tangents,\n\nvbardot_i += (∂φ_k/∂v_i) * vbardot_k + vbar_k * sum_j (∂²φ_k/∂v_i∂v_j) * dv_j.\n\nThe curvature term of each node comes from curvature_apply!, which every Curvature supplies in the form its structure allows — a PairedCurvature node reads its 2q nonzeros off the pairs rather than scanning a 2q × 2q matrix, without which a program built from q-long inner products would cost O(q) gradients instead of two. A node whose second partials are only declared (has_exact_partials is false) makes an exact product impossible: hvp throws on any tape containing one, unconditionally, rather than letting success depend on whether the node's adjoint happens to vanish. The tape method of scaled_spectral_radius inherits this restriction; its dense method does not.\n\nCosts about two gradients and O(L) memory.\n\nhvp! writes into Hv, which shares the axes of v. It saves the result allocation only: the two sweeps still allocate their own O(L) working vectors, so it is not allocation-free.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> f(x) = exp(x[1]) + x[1] * x[2]^2;\n\njulia> x0, v = [0.5, 2.0], [1.0, 0.0];\n\njulia> round.(hvp(f, x0, v); digits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 1.6487\n 4.0\n\njulia> h = cover_hessian(f, x0).h_sum;   # the cover bounds the product\n\njulia> all(abs.(hvp(f, x0, v)) .<= h .* (h' * abs.(v)))\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.hvp!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.jvp","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"jvp(t::Tape, outidxs::AbstractVector{<:Integer}, v::AbstractVector) -> Jv\njvp(r, x0::AbstractVector, v::AbstractVector) -> Jv\njvp!(Jv, t::Tape, outidxs::AbstractVector{<:Integer}, v::AbstractVector) -> Jv\n\nJacobian-vector product J*v of the taped vector-valued program, formed without the Jacobian by a single forward tangent sweep dv_k = sum_i (∂φ_k/∂v_i) dv_i seeded with v at the input nodes and read off at outidxs. Costs one sweep and O(L) time and memory, independent of the output count m — unlike adjoints, which is O(m*L).\n\nA repeated entry in outidxs yields an identical entry of Jv.\n\njvp! writes into Jv, which shares the axes of outidxs. It saves the result allocation only: the sweep still allocates its own O(L) working vector, so it is not allocation-free.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> r(x) = [x[1] * x[2], exp(x[1]) - x[3], x[2] + 2x[3]];\n\njulia> round.(jvp(r, [0.5, 2.0, -1.0], [1.0, 0.0, 1.0]); digits=4)\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 2.0\n 0.6487\n 2.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.jvp"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.jvp!","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"jvp(t::Tape, outidxs::AbstractVector{<:Integer}, v::AbstractVector) -> Jv\njvp(r, x0::AbstractVector, v::AbstractVector) -> Jv\njvp!(Jv, t::Tape, outidxs::AbstractVector{<:Integer}, v::AbstractVector) -> Jv\n\nJacobian-vector product J*v of the taped vector-valued program, formed without the Jacobian by a single forward tangent sweep dv_k = sum_i (∂φ_k/∂v_i) dv_i seeded with v at the input nodes and read off at outidxs. Costs one sweep and O(L) time and memory, independent of the output count m — unlike adjoints, which is O(m*L).\n\nA repeated entry in outidxs yields an identical entry of Jv.\n\njvp! writes into Jv, which shares the axes of outidxs. It saves the result allocation only: the sweep still allocates its own O(L) working vector, so it is not allocation-free.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> r(x) = [x[1] * x[2], exp(x[1]) - x[3], x[2] + 2x[3]];\n\njulia> round.(jvp(r, [0.5, 2.0, -1.0], [1.0, 0.0, 1.0]); digits=4)\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 2.0\n 0.6487\n 2.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.jvp!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.vjp","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"vjp(t::Tape, outidxs::AbstractVector{<:Integer}, u::AbstractVector) -> Jtu\nvjp(r, x0::AbstractVector, u::AbstractVector) -> Jtu\nvjp!(Jtu, t::Tape, outidxs::AbstractVector{<:Integer}, u::AbstractVector) -> Jtu\n\nVector-Jacobian product J'*u of the taped vector-valued program: one reverse adjoint sweep seeded with u[k] at node outidxs[k], read off at the input nodes. Costs one sweep and O(L) time and memory, independent of the output count m.\n\nOutputs that name the same node accumulate their seeds, so a repeated entry in outidxs contributes the sum of the corresponding entries of u — the transpose of jvp's duplicated rows.\n\nThe seed u shares the axes of outidxs, the mirror of jvp's Jv.\n\nvjp! writes into Jtu, which is indexed by the tape's inputs, Base.OneTo(t.ninputs). It saves the result allocation only: the sweep still allocates its own O(L) working vector, so it is not allocation-free.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> r(x) = [x[1] * x[2], exp(x[1]) - x[3], x[2] + 2x[3]];\n\njulia> round.(vjp(r, [0.5, 2.0, -1.0], [1.0, 1.0, 1.0]); digits=4)\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 3.6487\n 1.5\n 1.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.vjp"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.vjp!","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"vjp(t::Tape, outidxs::AbstractVector{<:Integer}, u::AbstractVector) -> Jtu\nvjp(r, x0::AbstractVector, u::AbstractVector) -> Jtu\nvjp!(Jtu, t::Tape, outidxs::AbstractVector{<:Integer}, u::AbstractVector) -> Jtu\n\nVector-Jacobian product J'*u of the taped vector-valued program: one reverse adjoint sweep seeded with u[k] at node outidxs[k], read off at the input nodes. Costs one sweep and O(L) time and memory, independent of the output count m.\n\nOutputs that name the same node accumulate their seeds, so a repeated entry in outidxs contributes the sum of the corresponding entries of u — the transpose of jvp's duplicated rows.\n\nThe seed u shares the axes of outidxs, the mirror of jvp's Jv.\n\nvjp! writes into Jtu, which is indexed by the tape's inputs, Base.OneTo(t.ninputs). It saves the result allocation only: the sweep still allocates its own O(L) working vector, so it is not allocation-free.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> r(x) = [x[1] * x[2], exp(x[1]) - x[3], x[2] + 2x[3]];\n\njulia> round.(vjp(r, [0.5, 2.0, -1.0], [1.0, 1.0, 1.0]); digits=4)\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 3.6487\n 1.5\n 1.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.vjp!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.adjoints","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"adjoints(t::Tape, outidx::Integer) -> vbar\n\nReverse-mode adjoints vbar[k] = ∂v_outidx/∂v_k for every node of t. The gradient of the program with respect to its inputs is vbar[1:t.ninputs], and cover_hessian(CoverOnly(), t, vbar) computes the Hessian cover from these adjoints without propagating them again.\n\n\n\n\n\nadjoints(t::Tape, outidxs::AbstractVector{<:Integer}) -> Vbar\n\nVector-mode reverse-mode adjoints for several outputs at once: Vbar[k, j] = ∂v_{outidxs[k]}/∂v_j, every output k against every node j, all propagated in one traversal. The m × n Jacobian of the outputs with respect to the inputs is Vbar[:, 1:t.ninputs].\n\nEach output owns a row seeded independently, so a repeated entry in outidxs yields identical rows and an input-node entry a unit row. Costs O(m*L) time and memory: a diagnostic path, like sensitivities, for the exact signed Jacobian; the production cover merges seed abs_reverse at the outputs instead and stay O(L)/O(G*L).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.adjoints"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.scaled_spectral_radius","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"scaled_spectral_radius(H::AbstractMatrix, h::AbstractVector) -> lambda\nscaled_spectral_radius(t::Tape, outidx::Integer, h::AbstractVector; maxiter=200, rtol=cbrt(eps(eltype(h))), rng=Xoshiro(0)) -> lambda\n\nSpectral norm of the scaled Hessian Hhat = D^-1 H D^-1, D = diag(h), which measures how loose the cover h is in the spectral sense: h is valid iff every entry of Hhat is at most 1 in magnitude, and lambda >= max_ij abs(Hhat[i,j]).\n\nBecause every entry of the symmetric Hhat is bounded by its spectral norm, abs(H[i,j]) <= lambda * h[i] * h[j], so sqrt(lambda) * h is also a cover of H. It is tighter than h when lambda < 1. lambda must bound the scaled norm wherever the cover is meant to hold: for a cover computed over a set of points, such as a box of intervals, that is the supremum over the set.\n\nThe dense method is exact. Coordinates with h[i] == 0 are admissible only if row i of H vanishes, in which case they are dropped; otherwise h does not cover H and an error is thrown.\n\nThe tape method is matrix-free: power iteration on Hhat driven by hvp, stopped when the estimate changes by less than rtol relative. It requires h to be strictly positive, and a tape whose element type is its own magtype: power iteration evaluates at a point, so a set-valued tape (such as one recorded on intervals) is an error. Each iterate norm(Hhat*v) with norm(v) == 1 is a lower bound on lambda and the sequence converges to it from below, so rescaling a cover by sqrt(safety * lambda) is valid only for a safety > 1 large enough to make safety * lambda an upper bound. Failure to converge within maxiter is an error, not a silently returned partial result.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.scaled_spectral_radius"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.scaled_singular_value","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"scaled_singular_value(J::AbstractMatrix, a::AbstractVector, b::AbstractVector) -> sigma\nscaled_singular_value(t::Tape, outidxs, a::AbstractVector, b::AbstractVector; maxiter=200, rtol=cbrt(eps(promote_type(eltype(a), eltype(b)))), rng=Xoshiro(0)) -> sigma\n\nLargest singular value of the equilibrated Jacobian Jhat = diag(a)^-1 J diag(b)^-1 (see equilibrate), which measures how loose the cover (a, b) is in the spectral sense: (a, b) is valid iff every entry of Jhat is at most 1 in magnitude, and sigma >= max_kj abs(Jhat[k,j]).\n\nBecause every entry of Jhat is bounded by its spectral norm, abs(J[k,j]) <= sigma * a[k] * b[j], so (sqrt(sigma) * a, sqrt(sigma) * b) is also a cover of J, tighter than (a, b) when sigma < 1. sigma must bound the scaled norm wherever the cover is meant to hold: for a cover computed over a set of points, such as a box of intervals, that is the supremum over the set.\n\nThe dense method is exact, and drops zero weights on the same terms as equilibrate.\n\nThe tape method is matrix-free: power iteration on Jhat'Jhat driven by jvp and vjp — about two gradients per iteration, O(L) memory — stopped when the estimate changes by less than rtol relative. It requires a and b to be strictly positive, and a tape whose element type is its own magtype: power iteration evaluates at a point, so a set-valued tape (such as one recorded on intervals) is an error. Each iterate norm(Jhat*v) with norm(v) == 1 is a lower bound on sigma and the sequence converges to it from below, so rescaling a cover by sqrt(safety * sigma) is valid only for a safety > 1 large enough to make safety * sigma an upper bound. Failure to converge within maxiter is an error, not a silently returned partial result.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.scaled_singular_value"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/covers/#DerivativeCovers.equilibrate","page":"Computing and using covers","text":"equilibrate(J::AbstractMatrix, a::AbstractVector, b::AbstractVector) -> Jhat\n\nThe two-sided equilibrated Jacobian Jhat = diag(a)^-1 J diag(b)^-1, in which the cover pair (a, b) becomes the all-ones covering matrix: (a, b) covers J if and only if every entry of Jhat is at most 1 in magnitude. The equilibrated matrix is the scale-invariant object a downstream linear method should see, since it is unchanged by any rescaling of the outputs or inputs that the cover tracks.\n\nA zero weight is admissible only if the row or column it scales vanishes, in which case that row or column of Jhat is zero; otherwise (a, b) does not cover J and an error is thrown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DerivativeCovers.equilibrate"},{"category":"section","location":"extending/#Programs-and-new-operations","page":"Programs and new operations","text":"","title":"Programs and new operations"},{"category":"section","location":"extending/#Writing-a-program","page":"Programs and new operations","text":"The functions that take a program f and a point x0 call f on a vector of TVars, which record each operation on a Tape (or, for forward_cover, on a vector of FVars, which propagate the cover as the program runs). f must therefore be written generically, and use only the supported operations:\n\narithmetic: +, -, *, /, inv, and ^ (with a constant or variable exponent);\nfunctions: abs, abs2, sqrt, exp, log, sin, cos, sincos, sinpi, cospi, sincospi, tan, tanh, atan, and two-argument atan(y, x) away from its branch cut (y = 0, x ≤ 0);\narray operations: sum, dot, and matrix-vector and matrix-matrix products, which record one operation per entry of the result rather than a sequence of scalar operations.\n\nConstants, including typed literals such as zero(eltype(x)), are folded into the operations that use them and take no space on the tape.\n\nComparisons are not defined for TVar. To branch on the value of a variable, compare its underlying value, obtained with value:\n\njulia> using DerivativeCovers\n\njulia> f(x) = DerivativeCovers.value(x[1]) > 0 ? x[1]^2 * x[2] : -x[1] * x[2];\n\njulia> round.(cover_hessian(f, [1.0, 2.0]).h_sum; digits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 4.8284\n 0.7071\n\nThe cover then describes the branch taken at x0.","title":"Writing a program"},{"category":"section","location":"extending/#Recording-a-tape","page":"Programs and new operations","text":"tape_function records a program and returns the tape and the index of the output node (or a vector of indices, for a vector-valued program). The tape can also be recorded by hand:\n\njulia> t = Tape([0.5, 2.0]);\n\njulia> x = [TVar(t, i) for i in 1:t.ninputs];\n\njulia> y = exp(x[1]) + x[1] * x[2]^2;\n\njulia> DerivativeCovers.nnodes(t)\n6\n\nThe element type of the tape, eltype(t), sets the precision of the whole computation. It defaults to float(eltype(x0)); use Tape{Float32}(x0) to record in another precision.","title":"Recording a tape"},{"category":"section","location":"extending/#Adding-an-operation","page":"Programs and new operations","text":"A new scalar operation is added by computing its value and its first and second derivatives and passing them to one of the recording functions unarynode or binarynode. Defining the method for CoverVar, the supertype of TVar and FVar, makes the operation available to both the tape and forward_cover:\n\njulia> function Base.log1p(x::DerivativeCovers.CoverVar)\n           v = DerivativeCovers.value(x)\n           return DerivativeCovers.unarynode(x, log1p(v), 1 / (1 + v), -1 / (1 + v)^2)\n       end;\n\njulia> g(x) = log1p(x[1] * x[2]);\n\njulia> round.(cover_hessian(g, [0.5, 2.0]).h_sum; digits=4)\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 2.4142\n 0.6036\n\nOperations with more than two arguments and structured second derivatives are recorded with pushnode!, which takes the second derivatives as a Curvature. The docstring of Curvature lists the methods a new form must implement. dotnode and linearnode record inner products and linear combinations.","title":"Adding an operation"},{"category":"section","location":"extending/#Declaring-a-sub-map","page":"Programs and new operations","text":"A part of the program that maps several values to several others can be recorded as a single operation per output, described by a declaration: the values of its outputs, its exact Jacobian, and a cover of the Hessian of each output. The tape then records the declaration instead of the operations inside the part.\n\nSuppose the program passes two intermediate values through a map F from mathbbR^2 to mathbbR^2. submap(F, xs) evaluates F on the arguments xs, computes the declaration, and records it on the tape of xs, returning the outputs of F:\n\njulia> using DerivativeCovers\n\njulia> F(v) = [v[1] * exp(v[2]), v[1]^2 + v[2]^2];\n\njulia> function outer(x)\n           y = DerivativeCovers.submap(F, [x[1] + x[2], x[3]])\n           return y[1] * y[2] + x[1]\n       end;\n\njulia> x0 = [0.3, 0.2, -0.4];\n\njulia> round.(cover_hessian(outer, x0).h_sum; digits=4)\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 3.2057\n 3.2057\n 2.6541\n\ndeclare_submap computes the same declaration without recording it. Here the arguments of F at x0 are [0.5, -0.4]:\n\njulia> d = DerivativeCovers.declare_submap(F, [0.5, -0.4]);\n\njulia> round.(d.J; digits=4)     # J[k, i] is the derivative of output k with respect to argument i\n2×2 Matrix{Float64}:\n 0.6703   0.3352\n 1.0     -0.8\n\njulia> round.(d.S; digits=4)     # row k is a cover of the Hessian of output k\n2×2 Matrix{Float64}:\n 1.1579  1.1579\n 1.4142  1.4142\n\nsubmapnode records a declaration supplied by the caller. This allows a part of the program that cannot be recorded, such as a call to an external library, to appear on the tape, provided that its Jacobian can be computed and its second derivatives bounded by other means. Covers computed from the tape are valid provided that the declaration is. Because the exact second derivatives are not recorded, hvp is not available on a tape that contains a declared sub-map.","title":"Declaring a sub-map"},{"category":"section","location":"extending/#Combining-covers-of-pieces","page":"Programs and new operations","text":"When a program is a composition F₂ ∘ F₁ and covers of the two pieces are available separately, compose_jacobian_cover combines Jacobian covers of F₁ and F₂ into a cover of the Jacobian of the composition, and compose_hessian_cover combines covers of the derivatives of F and g into a cover of the Hessian of g ∘ F:\n\njulia> using DerivativeCovers\n\njulia> F1(x) = [x[1] * x[2], x[1] + x[2]];\n\njulia> F2(y) = [exp(y[1]) * y[2]];\n\njulia> x0 = [0.5, 2.0];\n\njulia> y0 = [x0[1] * x0[2], x0[1] + x0[2]];\n\njulia> c1 = cover_jacobian(F1, x0); c2 = cover_jacobian(F2, y0);\n\njulia> a, b = DerivativeCovers.compose_jacobian_cover(c2.a, c2.b_sum, c1.a, c1.b_sum);\n\njulia> J = jvp(x -> F2(F1(x)), x0, [1.0, 0.0])[1], jvp(x -> F2(F1(x)), x0, [0.0, 1.0])[1];\n\njulia> all(abs.(collect(J)) .<= a[1] .* b)\ntrue\n\nThis requires no joint recording of the two pieces, so the pieces can be covered by different means or over different regions (see Covers over a box).","title":"Combining covers of pieces"}]
}
